  ('toddlerhood', 1, 1, 'what-changes-between-one-and-three', 'What Changes Between One and Three', 'draft', $t$Picture a two-year-old standing over the dog's water bowl. She looks straight at her father, shakes her head, and tells herself no. Then she plunges both hands into the water anyway.

Welcome to the Toddler Years class. In this first lesson, we'll look at what grows fastest between one and three, and at what lags behind. We'll see why a toddler can know a rule and still not follow it, and we'll finish with a question that makes these years easier.

If you took the Infant class, you heard that the brain about doubles in size during the first year. Growth slows after that, but it certainly doesn't stop.

In one study that scanned children from birth to age two, the brain grew by another fifteen percent in the second year.

A second study followed seventy-two children with scans soon after birth, at one, and at two. In the second year, the areas for basic sensing grew more slowly, while areas toward the front and top of the brain grew faster. The brain uses those areas for planning, attention, and control, and they still have a long way to go.

The change you can see most easily is movement. As the Infant class covered, most children take their first steps alone somewhere between about eight and eighteen months.

Once they start, the practice is enormous. Researchers who watched toddlers between twelve and nineteen months at play counted an average of about twenty-four hundred steps and seventeen falls an hour.

All that practice pays off quickly, because new walkers soon took more steps, traveled farther, and fell less. A toddler who never sits still is doing exactly the work this age calls for.

Walking also changes how a child connects with you. One study followed twenty-five babies for seven months around the time of their first steps.

After they began to walk, their use of gestures grew much faster. They started walking over to a parent to show off a toy, and parents had more chances to answer and to talk about whatever had been carried over.

Some studies also find a jump in words around the time walking starts, though others don't. What is clear is that a walking child can bring the world to you, and that invites conversation.

Three other things take off in these years. Language grows from a handful of words to short sentences, and Module 2 is all about that.

Memory stretches in these years too. Even at fourteen months, a child can copy something a week after seeing it. By two, a memory can last for months, which we'll come to in Lesson 1.3.

And somewhere in the second year, children begin to recognize themselves and to talk about what is mine. That new sense of self is behind a lot of toddler behavior, and it's where Module 3 begins.

So movement, language, memory, and a sense of self all race ahead. Three skills trail well behind them, and they are stopping, waiting, and switching.

Stopping means holding back an action that is already on its way. Waiting means putting off something you want. Switching means letting go of one thing in order to move to another.

These skills aren't missing in a toddler. They are present in early, shaky forms, and they take years to mature. One study gave about a hundred children small tasks like waiting, slowing down, and lowering their voice. The children improved a great deal between about two and almost three.

Part of the reason lies in the brain's wiring. In the Infant class, we met myelin, the fatty coating that lets a pathway carry its signals faster.

That coating is not laid down everywhere at once. In brain scans of children from one to six, the areas for touch, movement, and vision were already well coated by about the first birthday.

Areas at the front and sides of the brain took far longer. Those include areas used for language and for self-control, and a review of research on the brain's wiring finds it still maturing into young adulthood.

Now put those pieces together. A toddler has a body that can run and climb, strong wants, and a growing memory for what she wants.

The part of the brain that helps hold a rule in mind and stop an action sits right behind the forehead. It is growing fast in these years, and yet it is among the last parts of the brain to finish.

So the engine arrives years before the brakes are ready. The next lesson looks closely at those brakes, and at the alarm that sets a toddler off.

Here is how that gap looks in an experiment. Researchers gave children around two and a half a rule for sorting pictures into two boxes. Things found inside a house went in one box, and things found outside went in the other.

When the children were asked about each picture, they did well at saying whether it belonged inside or outside. When they had to use the rule and sort the pictures themselves, the younger ones did worse.

By three, knowing and doing matched. In a follow-up with a hundred and twenty children, the mistakes had a pattern, because children tended to repeat whatever they had just done.

Researchers still debate what causes this. Some think the knowledge is solid and the control over action is weak. Others think the knowledge itself is still faint, strong enough to answer a question and too weak to steer a busy pair of hands.

Either way, the lesson for parents is the same. There is a gap between knowing the rule and being able to follow it, and it shows up clearly in the toddler years.

Think of the girl at the water bowl. She knew the rule well enough to say it out loud, and her hands went in anyway. She wasn't being sneaky, and she wasn't testing her father.

A playful study shows a similar gap from another side. Researchers gave children between eighteen and thirty months miniature versions of familiar things, like a dollhouse chair and a tiny toy car.

Every so often, a child would make a serious attempt to sit down on the tiny chair, or to climb into the tiny car. It was an honest try and not a game.

Researchers think the plan to act ran ahead of the information about size. Not every toddler does this, but it shows how loosely seeing, knowing, and doing are tied together at this age.

Some requests are harder than others. One study followed a hundred and eight children from fourteen months to almost four, and compared two kinds of request.

In one, the mother asked her child not to touch some very attractive toys. In the other, she asked her child to keep going with a dull job, like putting toys away.

At every age, sticking with the dull job was much harder than leaving the toys alone. So expect cleanup to need your help for years, long after a simple don't touch has started to work.

For the youngest toddlers, there is an even simpler reason a rule isn't followed. They may not understand the words yet.

In a small study of thirty children between twelve and eighteen months, following a request went hand in hand with understanding it. The biggest jump came between about fifteen and eighteen months.

Another study asked mothers which rules their young children followed best. Safety rules, and rules about other people's things, came out on top. It may be no accident that those are the rules parents keep short and never bend.

So what does this mean at home? Start by expecting the gap. A rule your toddler can recite still needs you to carry it for a while.

Go close, get down to eye level, and use very few words. Then help the stop happen, by gently holding a hand, moving the bowl, or lifting your child away.

Where you can, change the room instead of testing the brakes. A gate, a high shelf, or a closed door will do more than a rule repeated from across the room.

Because stopping is so hard, give those hands something to do instead. Telling a toddler to stop leaves an empty space, and handing her a cup to fill at the sink gives her a new plan.

For jobs like cleanup, work together and keep it short. Hand over one block at a time, or make it a game of filling the basket.

And expect to repeat yourself, calmly, for months. Repeating a rule is not a sign that it isn't working, because at this age you are part of how the rule works.

When a toddler breaks a rule she clearly knows, it is natural to ask why she keeps doing this. That question tends to lead to answers like stubborn, or doing it on purpose.

Those answers change how we act. In one experiment, some mothers of toddlers were told that their child's misbehavior would be deliberate, and others were told their child was not to blame. The first group felt angrier and disciplined more harshly, and their children showed more upset.

A more useful question is what your child needs right now. Often the answer is help with stopping, or fewer words, or food, sleep, or a hug.

Here are three things to take with you.

Some skills race and some trail. Movement, words, memory, and wants surge between one and three, while stopping, waiting, and switching take years.

Knowing a rule comes before following it. A toddler can know a rule before she can follow it, so for now you are the brakes.

Ask what your child needs. When a rule gets broken, that question will take you further than asking why.

None of this means giving up on limits. Toddlers need them, and practicing with your help is part of how they learn, which is where Module 3 picks up.

In the next lesson, we'll look at why a toddler's feelings rise so fast, and at how stopping and waiting grow, and I'll see you in that lesson.$t$),
  ('toddlerhood', 1, 2, 'the-alarm-and-the-brakes', 'The Alarm and the Brakes', 'draft', $t$It's half past five, dinner is ten minutes away, and an eighteen-month-old is handed the blue cup when he wanted the green one. Within seconds he is flat on the kitchen floor, and nothing you say seems to reach him.

In this lesson, we'll meet two systems in the brain, a fast alarm and slow brakes. We'll look at what the alarm does to a toddler's body, and at how stopping and waiting grow from one to three. We'll also see why tired or overloaded toddlers fall apart sooner, and why some children hit the wall faster than others.

Deep in the brain sits a set of areas that work like an alarm. They notice a threat, a loss, or a blocked wish, and they launch a reaction in the body before any thinking is done.

At the front of the brain is the system we met in the last lesson. It works like brakes, slowing a reaction down so that a child can stop, wait, or switch.

This is a simplified picture, because the real brain works as connected networks and not as two separate boxes. Still, it explains a great deal about a toddler's day.

The alarm gets a head start. In brain scans of eighty children from three months to five years, many of the alarm's connections already looked much like an adult's from infancy.

A larger study of more than two hundred babies found that these connections grow dramatically in the first year, and are then fine-tuned in the second.

That doesn't mean the alarm is finished at birth, as parents are sometimes told. It keeps developing through childhood, but it is well connected long before the brakes have matured.

What comes later is the link between the alarm and the front of the brain. In that study of eighty children, the changes to this link that show up in older children had not yet appeared by age five.

One well-known study suggested that the link doesn't settle into its grown-up pattern until about ten. A later analysis found that result less solid, so the exact timing is still uncertain.

What the studies point to is the order. The alarm works early, the brakes come slowly, and the line between them is still being laid all through childhood.

When the alarm goes off, the body gets ready to act. The heart speeds up, muscles tighten, and stress hormones are released.

Those hormones are slow to rise. In one small study, toddlers between twelve and eighteen months were tested after mildly stressful events. Their main stress hormone was at its highest twenty minutes later in half of the children, and forty minutes later in the other half.

So a toddler's body can still be on alert well after the crying has stopped. Strong stress also makes the brakes work less well, as it does in adults. It is one reason that reasoning with a child in the middle of a meltdown rarely works, as we'll see in Module 4.

So how early do the brakes exist at all? Researchers gave a hundred and thirty-five ten-month-olds a simple touchscreen game. Tapping one spot usually brought a reward, and now and then the baby had to hold back and tap somewhere else.

The babies wore a soft cap that uses light to measure activity in the brain. When holding back was needed, areas on the right side of the brain, at the front and toward the top, grew more active.

Those are similar to the areas older children and adults use for stopping. So the brakes aren't missing in a baby, even though they have a long way to go.

The researchers saw the same children again at sixteen months. This time, a wider set of areas was at work, on both sides of the front of the brain.

Yet the toddlers were no better at the game than they had been at ten months. The brain was calling in more help without getting better results so far.

The researchers themselves say this finding needs to be repeated by others. When they tested the children once more at three and a half, they were clearly better at the game.

Between those ages, progress is steady but slow. On the same kind of game, other toddlers improved bit by bit at eighteen, twenty-one, and twenty-four months. A separate group did better at two and a half than at two.

Progress is uneven as well as slow. At ten and sixteen months, a child who held back well in one game often didn't in another, and doing well early said little about doing well later.

Early skill does begin to tell by two and a half. In a study that followed ninety-six children, self-control at two and a half was strongly tied to self-control at five. Even so, a toddler's brakes will hold in one moment and fail in the next, and that is normal.

Waiting shows the same slow growth. Researchers followed a hundred and twenty children from eighteen months to four years. At each age, the child had to wait eight minutes to open a gift while the mother was busy.

At eighteen months and at two, children were quick to get angry and slow to look away from the gift. By three, children typically turned to something else before showing anger, and their anger was shorter.

At four, a typical child spoke to her mother about the wait, and then found something to do. The big shift came between two and three.

What separates a toddler who manages to wait from one who doesn't? In a study of sixty-two two- and three-year-olds waiting for a snack and a gift, two things predicted success.

The children who managed looked away from the treat. They also kept their own hands back, for example by holding one hand with the other, and they usually started within the first ten seconds.

So for a toddler, waiting is mostly about where the eyes and hands go. A child staring at the cookie is using weak brakes at full strength, and will not last long.

Looking away can be learned by watching. In two experiments with children around two, some toddlers watched an adult handle a frustrating wait by turning to something else.

Afterward, those toddlers distracted themselves more during their own wait than children who had seen nothing. In one experiment it worked whether the adult was a parent or a stranger, and in the other, more distraction went along with less upset.

Parents can also offer it directly. In a study of a hundred and thirty-four mothers and two-year-olds, offering a distraction went with calmer waiting. Holding the child back, or brushing off the upset, went with stronger distress in the moments that followed.

Everything in this lesson gets harder when a toddler is worn down, and sleep is the best-studied example. The Preschool class describes experiments in which children close to three skipped a single nap. That afternoon they showed more negative feeling, and less mature coping with a frustrating puzzle. Two other small experiments found no clear change in holding back itself, so the effect seems to fall on mood and coping.

The same pattern shows up over months. In a study that followed a hundred and seventeen babies to eighteen months, later bedtimes and less sleep went with more anxious, clingy, and withdrawn behavior.

A study of more than four thousand Australian children found a loop. Sleep problems were followed by more trouble managing feelings, which was followed by more sleep problems. These are links over time and not proof, but they fit what most parents see.

Hunger has been studied far less in toddlers, so here we lean mostly on what parents see every day. A small snack before a hard stretch of the day is cheap insurance.

Noise and disorder have been studied more. Across thirty-five studies of more than sixteen thousand children, homes that were more disorganized and less stable went with slightly weaker self-control, and the link was small.

In one study, two-year-olds without regular routines were worse at waiting when they were five. Noise and crowding made no difference once routines were counted. So a predictable day seems to matter more than a quiet or roomy house.

Some children reach their limit sooner than others. As the Infant class covered, babies differ in temperament from the start, and some react more strongly to the very same event.

There are small average differences between boys and girls too. Across more than a hundred studies of children from one to nine, girls were slightly better at waiting. The difference is small, and the two groups overlap a great deal.

A child who melts down fast at two is not giving you a forecast of who she will be. It usually means her alarm is quick, and that her brakes need more of your help for now.

Some toddlers are bothered far more than others by loud sounds, scratchy clothes, or messy hands. In a study of about a thousand pairs of two-year-old twins, these children also tended to be more fearful and quicker to get upset. The sensitivity also ran partly in families.

For most children, it eases. In a follow-up of nearly a thousand twins, about three in four of those who were highly sensitive at two no longer were at seven.

For a smaller group it lasts. In another study, strong sensitivity in the preschool years went with more anxiety at six. If sensitivities are getting in the way of eating, dressing, or going out, mention it to your child's doctor.

Go back to the kitchen floor at half past five. That boy was tired and hungry, the house was busy, and his alarm went off over a cup.

Nothing was wrong with him, and nothing was wrong with his parents. The phrase to retire is that he should know better by now, because knowing was never the problem.

What helps is working with both systems. Look after sleep, food, and a predictable rhythm before the hard parts of the day. When waiting is needed, turn eyes and hands toward something else. And once the alarm is ringing, your child needs to borrow your calm before anything else, which is the heart of Module 4.

Here are three things to take with you.

The alarm is early and the brakes are late. Feelings rise fast in a toddler because the system that slows them is still being built.

Worn-down toddlers cope less well. Enough sleep and a predictable day give them a better chance.

Looking away is the first waiting skill. You can offer a distraction, and you can show your child how it's done.

By three, a typical child can turn away from what she wants before the anger comes, and the preschool years are when the brakes really start to grow. Until then, a toddler needs to borrow yours.

In the next lesson, we'll look at how toddlers learn by watching, repeating, and remembering, and I'll see you in that lesson.$t$),
  ('toddlerhood', 1, 3, 'learning-by-watching-repeating-and-remembering', 'Learning by Watching, Repeating, and Remembering', 'draft', $t$A father takes a quick phone call at breakfast, pacing the kitchen as he talks. A week later, his twenty-month-old picks up a banana, holds it to her ear, and paces the same stretch of floor, chatting away.

In this lesson, we'll see how much toddlers learn by watching, and how long they remember it. We'll look at why they ask for the same book again and again, and at what a normal attention span looks like. We'll finish with what stress does to all this learning, and how you help.

Copying something long after you saw it is a big step, because it means holding a memory with no reminder in front of you. Researchers have tested this with a simple method.

In a classic study, fourteen-month-olds watched an adult do six things with six objects, including one action no baby would think of alone. The babies were not allowed to touch anything.

A week later, they came back and were handed the objects. They performed clearly more of the actions than babies who had never seen them, and that included the odd one.

How long a memory lasts grows quickly across these two years. In one set of experiments, eighteen-month-olds could copy a set of actions two weeks after seeing it.

Two-year-olds still remembered after eight weeks, and showed some signs of remembering at three months. By six months, the memory was gone.

Another study found that babies of fourteen and sixteen months could still copy several actions two months and even four months later. The numbers differ from study to study, depending on the task. Where ages were compared directly, though, older toddlers remembered for longer.

Early memories are also tied tightly to the exact thing a child saw. In one study, one-year-olds who had watched actions on one object often could not repeat them the next day on a slightly different one.

By eighteen months that kind of change no longer got in the way, and up to two and a half memories became more flexible still.

Giving things a name helps with this. When an adult named the object while showing what to do, and again later, two-year-olds were better at using what they had learned on a different object. So talking about what you are doing helps a memory travel.

Toddlers also learn from things that were never aimed at them. In one set of studies, two-year-olds were nearby while two adults talked to each other and used a made-up word for a new object.

Children of two and a half learned the new word just as well by listening in as when an adult taught it to them directly. Other studies found that eighteen-month-olds can learn words this way too, though less reliably at that age.

There is one condition worth knowing. In a small separate study, toddlers learned by listening in only when both adults were paying attention to the conversation. What they learn from is real talk between people who are engaged with each other.

Toddlers pick up feelings from the sidelines as well. In one experiment, eighteen-month-olds watched an adult play with a toy, and then saw a second adult react angrily to what the first had done.

When the toddlers were given the toy, they held back from copying, but only while the adult who had been angry could see them. If she left the room or turned her back, they went right ahead.

A study of a hundred and fifty children found the same pattern at fifteen months. So a toddler already tracks who is upset by what, and who is looking. Notice how much the holding back depended on being watched, which fits what we saw about the brakes in the last lesson.

That banana held up as a phone brings several of these skills together. It is a memory of something watched, replayed later, with one object standing in for another.

When researchers followed ninety-eight babies through the second year, copying from memory came first. Pretend play appeared later, mostly between eighteen and twenty-one months.

Early pretending usually copies a child's own day, like drinking from an empty cup. Then it turns outward to feeding a doll, and then one object begins to stand in for another. The Preschool class picks up the story of pretend play from three to five.

Put this together and one thing follows. Much of what a toddler learns in a day comes from watching the people she loves do ordinary things.

She studies how you sweep, how you greet the neighbor, and how you speak to the dog. She also studies what you do when you are frustrated, which the last lesson showed can be copied too.

This isn't a reason to put on a performance. Nobody is calm and kind all day, and toddlers also learn from watching you recover and make things right, which we'll come to in Module 4.

Now to the book you have read forty times this month. There is some evidence that all that asking is doing your child good.

In one study, three-year-olds heard stories that contained two made-up words. Some children heard three different stories, and others heard the same story three times, so every child heard each new word equally often.

The children who heard the same story again and again learned the new words well. The children who heard different stories did not end up learning them. That study was with three-year-olds, and younger toddlers have been studied less.

Why would the same story teach more? One likely reason is that once a story is familiar, a child no longer has to work out what is happening. That leaves more attention for a new word or a new detail.

Repetition also suits a toddler's memory. As we saw, a toddler's memory of something seen once fades within weeks or months, and it is likely that meeting it again helps keep it alive.

The Infant class said to let repetition be the curriculum, and that still holds. The same song, the same game, and the same book are how a toddler makes knowledge stick, and Module 2 has more on reading with a toddler.

Parents often worry that their toddler can't stick with anything. Researchers who have watched children at play find that deep, focused attention grows gradually through these years.

In one study, children were watched at ten months, at about two, and at three and a half. With age, casual looking gave way to more focused attention, and the youngest were the most easily pulled away.

So a one-year-old who flits from toy to toy is behaving like a one-year-old. You may see charts that promise a set number of minutes for each year of age. Those are rules of thumb and not research findings, because attention depends so much on the activity.

A toddler's attention is also not hers alone. Researchers put tiny cameras that track the eyes on one-year-olds and their parents while they played with toys.

When a parent looked at the toy the child was already looking at, the child stayed with it longer. When the parent also touched the toy or talked about it, the child's look lasted longer still, and talk went with the longest looks.

A later study added a twist. Talk that responded to what the child was doing stretched attention, and parents who talked the most overall got less out of each remark. So join in with what has already caught your child's eye.

A few things reliably shorten a toddler's focus. As the Infant class covered, a television left on in the background breaks up play, even when a child barely glances at it.

That class also described a study where toddlers played longer and more inventively with four toys than with sixteen. Interrupting works against you too, because each time an absorbed child is pulled to something else, that stretch of focus ends.

So you don't need to train attention. Protect the stretches that happen, by keeping the room calm, offering fewer things, and waiting a moment before you step in.

All of this learning goes better when a child feels safe. The Infant class described how a trusted adult's presence can soften the body's stress response, and called that adult a buffer.

Studies of toddlers add a detail to that picture. In one study, seventy-seven eighteen-month-olds met a series of strange new things with their mothers present. The stress hormone rose only in toddlers who were both cautious by nature and less secure in their bond with their mother.

Cautious toddlers with a secure bond showed no such rise, and a second study found a similar pattern at a doctor visit. These are links and not proof, but they suggest that a steady relationship changes what a scary thing does inside the body.

The Infant class also set out three kinds of stress. There is the brief, everyday kind, and the more serious kind that a child gets through with support. Then there is the severe, lasting kind without support, often called toxic stress, which is the one linked to harm.

Almost all of toddler life is the first kind. A hard puzzle, a short wait, a goodbye at the door, and a first haircut are small stresses that build coping when you are nearby.

So the aim is not a toddler who never feels stress. Your job is to be close enough that small stresses stay small, and that is something an ordinary, imperfect parent already does.

Parents also read that child care raises stress hormones, so here is what the studies say. On average, a toddler's stress hormone rises a little across a day in group care, and falls across a day at home.

The effect is small, and it varies. In a study of three hundred and twenty toddlers, those in disorganized groups showed clearly more stress. A Finnish study even found higher overall levels in toddlers who were cared for at home.

No one has shown that this mild rise harms children, and researchers suspect it reflects the effort of a long day in a group. What seems to help is a warm bond with the caregiver, a predictable day, and a calm reunion with you, and Lesson 4.2 covers drop-offs.

Here are three things to take with you.

Your toddler is studying you. She learns from what she watches and overhears, and she can replay it days or weeks later.

Again is how toddlers learn. Repeating the same book, song, or game is doing real work.

Join in and stay close. Attention grows when you follow your child's focus, and small stresses stay small when you are near.

That brings us to the end of Module 1. A toddler's brain is racing ahead in some ways and waiting on its brakes in others, and through all of it, your child is learning from you.

In the next lesson, we'll begin Module 2 with the word spurt, and with how wide the range of normal is, and I'll see you in that lesson.$t$),
  ('toddlerhood', 2, 1, 'the-word-spurt-and-how-wide-normal-is', 'The Word Spurt, and How Wide Normal Is', 'draft', $t$Two cousins, both twenty months old, are playing at a family dinner. One is asking for more juice and announcing that the dog is gone. The other says about ten words and gets everything else done by pointing, and both sets of parents are quietly comparing.

In this lesson, we'll follow language from first words to first little sentences. We'll look at the famous word spurt, and at how wide the range of normal really is. We'll finish with what an early word count can tell you, and what it can't.

Most children say their first words around their first birthday, and then, for a while, not much seems to happen.

It helps to know what counts as a word at this age, because it doesn't need to sound right. If your child says ba every time she wants her ball, and only then, that is a word.

The checklists that American doctors use give a floor for this stage. At least three in four children try one or two words besides mama and dada by fifteen months, and three or more by eighteen months. Those figures mark the low end of the range, since most children are already past them.

Underneath that slow start, a lot is going on. The Infant class showed that understanding comes before talking, and in the toddler years the gap between the two is easy to see.

One small study followed eight babies closely and listed the first fifty words they understood and the first fifty they said. The children understood fifty words at around thirteen months. They did not say fifty words until about eighteen months.

Understanding was also growing about twice as fast as talking. So a toddler with ten spoken words may be carrying a much bigger stock of words she knows, and it pays to notice what your child follows as well as what she says.

Around eighteen months, the pace changes. Across large collections of parent reports, most children say fewer than a hundred words at eighteen months. By their second birthday, a typical child says roughly three hundred.

That is more than a hundred and fifty new words in six months, which works out to nearly one new word a day. The first fifty words took about half a year.

This speeding up is what people call the word spurt, or the vocabulary explosion. It raises an obvious question about what suddenly switched on.

For a long time, the answer seemed to be that something clicks at around eighteen months. When researchers tracked children one by one, the picture turned out to be less tidy.

In one study of eighteen children, thirteen showed a burst of new words, mostly names for things, that lasted up to three months. The other five built their vocabularies more gradually.

A later study of thirty-eight children used a statistical test of whether each child's growth had a true turning point. Only five of them showed one. For the rest, word learning sped up smoothly, more like a ramp than a step.

So why does word learning speed up at all? One explanation needs no special switch. A child is working on many words at the same time, and words differ in how hard they are to learn.

A few words are easy, perhaps because a child hears them constantly and what they point to is obvious. Many more are harder and take longer. The easy few arrive first, one at a time. Then the large group of harder words, which the child has been working on all along, starts to arrive together.

A computer simulation built on only those two ideas produced the same slow start and the same speeding up. You can picture a snowball rolling downhill, which stays small for a long while and then grows fast.

Now back to those two cousins. The best-known survey of early vocabulary found that the spread between children is very wide.

It gathered parent reports on about eighteen hundred children. At two years old, a child in the middle of the group said roughly three hundred words. About one child in ten said fewer than sixty, and about one in ten said more than five hundred.

Children across most of that spread are developing in the ordinary way. The researchers concluded that a spread this wide challenges the idea of a single typical child.

Parents often hear that girls talk earlier than boys, or that first children talk earlier than later ones. Both have some truth to them, and both matter much less than you might think.

A study combining nearly fourteen thousand children from ten language communities found girls slightly ahead of boys in gestures, in spoken words, and in combining words. In the survey of eighteen hundred children, though, whether a child was a boy or a girl explained only one or two percent of the differences between children.

Birth order and family background explained even less at this age. So knowing that a child is a boy, or a third child, tells you almost nothing about how many words he will have.

A child growing up with two languages needs a slightly different count. In one study of toddlers from about two to two and a half, children learning two languages knew fewer words in each language than children learning only one.

When the researchers added the two languages together, the totals were comparable. How many words a child had in each language went along with how much of that language she heard.

So if your toddler hears two languages, count the words in both. Hearing two languages does not cause a language delay, and we'll come back to that later in this module. The Preschool class looks at two languages from three to five.

While the spoken words pile up, something less visible is changing too. Toddlers get much faster at understanding the words they already know.

Researchers measure this by showing a child two pictures, naming one of them, and filming where her eyes go. At fifteen months, children did not look to the right picture until the word was over.

By two, they were moving their eyes to the right picture before the word had even finished. They were working out the word from its first sounds, which is how adults listen.

That speed turns out to matter. In one study, children were tested this way at eighteen months and then followed for a year. The ones who were quicker to recognize familiar words went on to add vocabulary faster, up to two and a half.

The study included a group of toddlers who were saying very few words at eighteen months. Among them, the quicker ones were more likely to bloom and pick up speed over the following year.

So two toddlers with the same short list of spoken words can be in quite different places underneath. It is one more reason that a word count tells you only part of the story.

The next big step is putting two words together, as in more milk, daddy shoe, or doggie gone. Most children start somewhere between eighteen months and two years.

In one large set of parent reports, the girl in the middle of the group was combining words at about twenty months, and the boy in the middle at about twenty-two months. By their second birthday, at least three in four children say two words together.

Combining goes hand in hand with vocabulary. Across thousands of toddlers, the children with more words were the ones putting them together. These first sentences leave out the little words, and that is normal at this age.

The Infant class gave the main answer to what helps words grow, which is that back-and-forth conversation matters more than the sheer number of words a child hears. It also explained why the well-known figure of a thirty-million-word gap was not supported when it was tested again.

The toddler studies add detail. One followed families with all-day recordings at home. The children who heard more speech addressed directly to them were faster at understanding words, and had bigger vocabularies at two.

Speech that was simply going on around them did not predict vocabulary in that study. Toddlers can pick up a word by listening in, as we saw in the last lesson, but here it was talk aimed at the child that went with growth.

A second study looked closely at sixty lower-income families when the children were two. The researchers rated whether parent and child were sharing attention on the same thing, whether they had familiar routines and games, and whether the conversation flowed back and forth.

Those qualities at two explained about a quarter of the differences in children's spoken language a year later. They predicted considerably better than the number of words the mother said.

Thousands of all-day recordings add one more point. Among families of similar income and education, homes differ a great deal in how much back-and-forth a child gets, and the next lesson is about how to do it.

Parents naturally wonder what a word count at two says about the future. Vocabulary at two does predict later language and reading. One study of more than a thousand children found the link still there in fifth grade.

Two large Danish studies found the same for reading in sixth grade and for school exams at fifteen, but the link is a modest one. In the exam study, everything known about a child before three, including vocabulary and family background, explained between about a tenth and a fifth of the differences at fifteen.

So most of the story is still unwritten at two. An early talker is not guaranteed anything, and a slower start does not settle anything, although very low counts do carry more risk.

What should you do with all this? Start by treating word counts as a rough guide. A list on the fridge can be fun, but comparing your child with a cousin or a neighbor will mostly mislead you.

Watch the direction more than the number. Look for new words appearing from month to month, for your child following more of what you say, and for pointing and gestures that keep the conversation going.

If the words are few and those other signs are missing too, or if your instinct says something is off, that deserves a closer look. The last lesson in this module is about exactly when to check and how to ask.

Here are three things to take with you.

The word spurt is a snowball. Words come slowly at first and then speed up, and for many children it is a smooth ramp with no sudden switch.

Normal is very wide at two. Some two-year-olds say well under a hundred words, and others say several hundred.

Watch understanding and direction together. What your child follows, and whether new words keep coming, adds a lot to any single count.

So from first words to first sentences, toddlers follow the same path at very different speeds. Understanding leads, talking follows, and the talk aimed at your child helps to feed it.

In the next lesson, we'll look at the kinds of everyday talk that grow language, and at what you can skip, and I'll see you in that lesson.$t$),
  ('toddlerhood', 2, 2, 'talk-that-grows-language', 'Talk That Grows Language', 'draft', $t$A toddler at the window spots the garbage truck and shouts truck. Her father comes over and says yes, a big green truck, and it's lifting the bin. She watches for a moment, and then she says big truck.

In this lesson, we'll look at the kinds of everyday talk that help language grow, like that small exchange at the window. We'll cover responding, adding a little, routines, books, and songs. We'll finish with what you can happily skip.

The Infant class introduced serve and return, the back-and-forth in which a baby does something and you respond. With a toddler, the serves become words, points, and half-words, and your response still matters.

One study followed forty children from nine months to twenty-one months. Mothers who responded more to what their children were doing and saying had children who tended to reach language steps sooner. That included first words, the first fifty words, putting words together, and talking about the past.

Two features of a helpful response stand out. It comes quickly, and it is about the thing your child is already focused on.

Conversation is turn-taking, and toddlers are slow to take their turn. A review of twenty-six studies found that young children take about a second to respond in a conversation.

A second is a long silence between adults, so it is tempting to jump in and answer for them.

So after you say something to your toddler, wait. Count slowly to two in your head before you fill the gap, because the answer may be on its way. The Infant class called this waiting, and it still helps now.

When your toddler says a word or two, you say it back with a little more. Dog becomes yes, the dog is running. More juice becomes you want more juice. Researchers call this an expansion.

One study followed seventy-seven families from fifteen months to four and a half years. The more of a child's remarks the mother expanded between two and almost three, the faster the child's vocabulary grew. Those children also understood more words at four and a half.

A Spanish study found a similar pattern. How often adults repeated a two-year-old's words back, usually in a longer form, predicted the child's language at two and a half.

Toddlers make charming mistakes, like me do it, or two foots. You don't need to correct these, and parents often do something gentler. A child says I goed outside, and the parent answers yes, you went outside.

A close study of five children between two and four found that adults did this more often after a mistake than after a correct sentence. The children sometimes repeated the fixed part in their next turn, or said yes and carried on.

This kind of saying it back is called a recast, and it has also been tested as a therapy. A review of studies with children who have language difficulties found a benefit for their grammar, though the evidence is still limited.

Notice what is missing from both of those moves, which is that nobody asks the child to repeat anything. Many parents feel they should finish with say dog, or can you say truck.

The Spanish study looked at something close to this. How often the adults said things back predicted later language, but how often the children repeated the adults of their own accord did not.

Some parents also shorten their own speech to match, saying things like doggie run or shoe on. A small study of children with language delay tested this with phrases for the children to copy. The clipped phrases gave no advantage, and some children used more of the little words after hearing whole sentences. So there is no need to clip your sentences.

The Infant class showed that babies learn words better when you talk about what they are already looking at. With toddlers, the same rule covers telling them what to do.

In one small study, researchers sorted mothers' instructions to their thirteen-month-olds into two kinds. Some followed what the child was already doing, like push the car when the child was holding it. Others pulled the child toward something new. Only the first kind went along with a bigger vocabulary nine months later.

A second study, from ten to twenty-one months, found that intrusive instructions, the kind that push a child's attention elsewhere, went along with slower vocabulary growth. So your toddler's interest is the lesson plan.

You may not need a special language time, because the ordinary day supplies variety of its own. One study filmed forty mothers at home with their one-year-olds and sorted their talk by what was going on.

The amount of talk, the range of words, and what the talk was about all changed with the activity. Feeding, grooming, playing with toys, and sharing a book each brought their own kind of language.

An older study of toddlers found that the setting shaped what mothers said as well. So a toddler who is with you through the day's routines is hearing many kinds of talk. Variety of words matters at this age, and that is a thread the Preschool class picks up.

Books add something that conversation alone doesn't. One team compared the text of a hundred picture books with the same amount of everyday talk to young children, and the books contained more different words.

Books also seem to change how adults talk. In a study of sixty-nine fathers from low-income families and their two-year-olds, the fathers used a wider range of words and asked more questions while reading than while playing with toys. Those same features went with bigger vocabularies in their children.

And in trials where parents were coached to share books, children's spoken vocabulary improved by a modest amount. Most of those children had language difficulties or were at risk of them.

Reading with a toddler rarely looks like reading. She turns three pages at once, goes backward, points at the same duck six times, and walks off halfway through.

All of that is fine. Talking about the pictures and about what interests her is a good way to share a book. Name what she points at, and add a little to what she says. Ask a question she can answer, like where is the duck, and then wait. You don't have to read every printed word.

When she wants the same book again, say yes, for the reasons we saw in the last lesson. The Preschool class teaches a fuller method for reading together, and what you do now is where it begins.

Parents often ask whether songs and rhymes help language. The honest answer is that the research is thin but encouraging.

A few small studies have asked parents how much they sing with their babies and young toddlers, and then measured language. Children who were sung to more tended to understand more words and use more gestures, and in one study they said more words too.

Studies like these can't show that the singing caused the difference. Still, a song is slow, repeated, and shared face to face. So sing because you both enjoy it, and count any language benefit as a bonus.

Now for what you can skip, starting with flashcards and word drills. There is very little research on flashcards for toddlers, and nothing solid showing that they build vocabulary.

The nearest study compared two ways of teaching new words to two-year-olds. Children who were shown picture cards learned fewer words than children who used a word-learning app, especially an app shared with an adult. Across the study, the group of children who stayed focused for longest learned the most new words.

So interest is the thing to protect. A toddler is likely to learn more from a dog, a book, or a walk that has caught her attention than from a card.

You can also skip three other things. The first is correcting, because saying it back the right way gives her the right form without stopping the conversation.

The second is videos sold as teaching words. The Infant class covered a study in which children who watched one for a month learned no more words than children who never saw it. In that study, the most learning happened when parents taught the words during everyday activities.

The third is talking nonstop. The Infant class said that you don't need to narrate all day, and that holds here. Quiet stretches of play are fine.

What helps most shifts a little across these two years. One study followed a hundred and forty-six mothers and children at fourteen months, at two years, and at three years.

The researchers separated two things. One was sensitivity, meaning how well a mother read and responded to her child. The other was stimulation, meaning how much she taught, explained, and added. At fourteen months, sensitivity mattered more for vocabulary, and the effect of stimulation grew across the toddler years.

So respond first, and add more as your child's language grows.

None of this takes a special talent, and that has been tested. Researchers have run many trials in which parents were coached in habits like these.

One review combined eighteen studies of children from one and a half to five who had language difficulties. Another combined twenty-five trials with more than seventeen hundred children, most of whom had language difficulties or were at risk of them. Both found that when parents were coached, children's language improved.

The gains were modest, which is worth saying plainly. In the larger review, they showed up in what children said more than in what they understood. So these are habits that can be taught, and they make a difference you can measure.

Here is how it might look across one day. At breakfast, your toddler says nana, and you say you want a banana, so here's your banana.

While she is getting dressed, you follow her eyes to the sock in her hand and tell her that's your sock, and it goes on your foot. On a walk, she points at a bus, and you name it and wait.

At bath time you sing the same song as yesterday, and before bed you open the same book and talk about the duck. That makes five small moments, and none of them took extra time. On the days when you are tired and quiet, don't worry, because it is the pattern over months that counts.

Here are three things to take with you.

Respond first, and then wait. Answer what your toddler starts, and leave a second or two for her turn.

Say it back with a little more. Expanding and gently fixing what she says goes with faster language growth, and you can skip the correcting and quizzing.

Use the day you already have. Meals, dressing, play, and books each bring their own words, so you can skip the flashcards.

So the talk that grows language is ordinary talk, aimed at your child, about what she is doing, with room for her to answer. You were probably doing much of it already.

In the next lesson, we'll look at late talking, at how to tell when it is worth checking, and at how to ask for help, and I'll see you in that lesson.$t$),
  ('toddlerhood', 2, 3, 'late-talkers-and-when-to-check', 'Late Talkers and When to Check', 'draft', $t$At her son's two-year checkup, a mother mentions that he says about fifteen words. His grandmother points out that his father talked late too, and he turned out fine. A friend says to wait until he is three, and the mother isn't sure whose advice to take.

In this lesson, we'll look at what late talking is, how often children catch up, and what tilts the odds. We'll cover hearing, ear infections, and two languages. We'll finish with when to check, and how to ask for an evaluation.

Researchers use the term late talker for a toddler, usually between eighteen months and two and a half, who says far fewer words than other children the same age and has no other known difficulty.

On one well-tested checklist that parents fill in, a two-year-old counts as a late talker with fewer than fifty words, or with no two-word combinations yet.

Other studies simply take the lowest tenth of children on a vocabulary checklist. Either way, the term describes where a child is today. It is not a diagnosis, and it says nothing yet about why.

Late talking is common, at roughly one toddler in eight. A study of nearly eighteen hundred Australian two-year-olds and a Canadian study of about a thousand families both found it in about thirteen percent.

Two things raised the odds in both studies. One was being a boy, and the other was having a relative who talked late or had a speech or language difficulty.

In the Australian study, parents' education, income, mental health, and parenting style were not linked to late talking. Other studies have found some links with family circumstances, but in one large project everything the researchers measured explained no more than seven percent of the differences at two. So late talking is usually not a sign that a parent did something wrong.

Here is the reassuring part. A British study followed more than eight thousand twins. It took the children in the lowest tenth for vocabulary at two, and looked at them again at three and at four.

At three, a little under half still had language difficulties, and at four it was about four in ten. So roughly six in ten had moved out of difficulty by four.

A review of the longer studies agrees. Most late talkers score in the average range on language tests by the time they are five, six, or seven.

The same studies carry a second message. As a group, late talkers stay a little behind children who talked on time, even after they reach the average range.

One study followed twenty-six late talkers to seventeen. They scored in the average range on every language and reading test, but lower than a matched group on vocabulary, grammar, and memory for words.

A study of ninety-six hundred American children found that late talkers at two were more likely to have a small vocabulary at four, and to be less ready for school at five. For a minority the difficulty lasts, and the Preschool class describes that under the name developmental language disorder.

It would help to know in advance which toddlers will catch up. Unfortunately, nobody can reliably tell for any one child.

In the twin study, the researchers tried to predict which two-year-olds would still be struggling at three and four. Their predictions were too inaccurate to be useful for any one child. The toddlers with the fewest words were not necessarily the ones whose difficulties lasted.

This is the real problem with waiting. The odds favor your child, but the odds describe a group, and you are raising one child.

A few signs do shift the odds, and one of the best supported is understanding. In a small early study, every late talker who was still behind a year later had also been behind in understanding at the start.

A review that combined twenty groups of late talkers, more than two thousand children, agreed. How much a toddler understood, and how many words she had, both predicted how she was talking in the preschool years.

Gestures are another sign, because in that small early study the children who stayed behind had also used fewer gestures. Family history raises the chance of talking late, but in the combined review it did not clearly predict who caught up, though some newer studies suggest it adds to the risk.

Whenever a toddler is slow to talk, the first thing to check is hearing. A child who passed the newborn hearing test can still have a hearing loss that began later, as the Infant class mentioned.

In one study in Shanghai, more than twenty-one thousand young children who had passed as newborns were screened again. Fewer than one in a thousand had a permanent hearing loss, though estimates vary widely between studies. Most of those children had no known risk factor, so nothing would have flagged them.

A hearing test for a toddler is painless, and a normal result rules hearing out as the cause for now.

Ear infections are the other hearing question, because the fluid that can stay behind the eardrum afterward muffles sound for a while.

A review that combined studies following children over time found either no link or a very small one between fluid in the ears in the early years and later speech and language.

In a large American trial, children under three with long-lasting fluid were given ear tubes either promptly or up to nine months later if the fluid was still there. The two groups did not differ in language or thinking up to age six, or in reading and schoolwork at nine to eleven. So in an otherwise healthy child, ear fluid is unlikely to be the whole explanation, though it is still worth raising with your doctor.

Families who use two languages are often told that this is why a toddler is slow to talk, but the research does not show that.

One study matched thirty-one toddlers learning two languages with thirty learning one. At thirteen months and at twenty months, the two groups did not differ in how many words they said. The authors suggested that when a child with two languages seems slow, the reason should be looked for somewhere else.

So count your child's words across both languages. If the total is low, treat it as you would for any child, and there is no need to drop a language.

Here are some markers to hold in mind. They come from the checklists American doctors use, which were revised to show what at least three in four children can do at each age.

By fifteen months, a child tries to say one or two words besides mama and dada. By eighteen months, she tries three or more words and follows a simple request without a gesture. By two, she puts two words together. By two and a half, she says about fifty words.

Since most children have reached each marker by that age, a missed one is not a reason to panic, but it is a reason to ask now.

A few signs are about more than words, and they deserve a prompt conversation with your doctor. One is a toddler who rarely points to show you things, and another is one who often doesn't turn to her name.

In one study of the baby brothers and sisters of autistic children, those later diagnosed with autism were more likely to miss their name from nine months on. A single missed call means very little, though, because plenty of children who never developed autism missed sometimes too.

The third sign is losing skills. If your child stops using words she had, or stops waving or pointing, call your doctor soon. In a large review, roughly three in ten autistic children lost skills this way. None of these signs is a diagnosis, and each is a reason to have someone look.

For years, the usual advice to a mother like the one in our story was to wait and see, because most late talkers do catch up.

That advice has been changing. When the American checklists were revised, one stated aim was to discourage waiting. Speech and language specialists have argued the same, because the children who won't catch up should not be overlooked, and nobody can reliably pick them out.

A national panel found too little evidence to say whether doctors should screen young children who show no signs and whose parents have no concerns. If you have a concern, you are already past that point, and asking is the sensible step.

Help for a late-talking toddler often means coaching for you. A specialist shows you the moves from the last lesson and helps you use them often.

In a German trial, mothers of two-year-olds who had few words but understood well took a three-month course. At three, three quarters of their children were talking in the normal range, compared with fewer than half of the children who waited.

Coaching doesn't fix everything, though. In an American trial with toddlers who were behind in both understanding and talking, it improved understanding but not talking, at least in the short term. Early help is worth having, and it is not a guarantee.

Start with your child's doctor. Say plainly what you have noticed, ask for a hearing test, and ask about seeing a speech and language specialist.

In the United States there is a second door. Every state has an early intervention program for children under three. You can call it yourself and ask for an evaluation. You don't need a referral or a diagnosis, and the evaluation is free.

From age three, the same request goes to your local public school, and the Preschool class covers checks from three to five. In other countries, your doctor or health visitor is the place to start. Wherever you are, you can simply say that you have concerns about your child's language and would like an evaluation.

While you wait for an appointment, keep doing what the last lesson described.

Try not to push for words. Accept a point or a sound as a real turn in the conversation, and give the word back.

And go easy on yourself. The Australian study found no link with parents' education or parenting style, and the clearest risks were being a boy and family history. Asking for a check does not mean that something is wrong, because it is simply how you find out.

Here are three things to take with you.

Most late talkers do catch up. About six in ten are out of difficulty by four, though nobody can reliably tell in advance which ones.

Understanding matters as well as talking. A toddler who follows what you say and uses gestures has better odds, and hearing should always be checked.

If you're worried, ask now. In the United States, you can request a free evaluation yourself before age three, with no referral.

That brings us to the end of Module 2. Words arrive at very different speeds, everyday talk feeds them, and when you are unsure, checking costs little while waiting can cost time.

In the next lesson, we'll begin Module 3 with the words mine and no, and with what they tell us about a toddler's new sense of self, and I'll see you in that lesson.$t$),
  ('toddlerhood', 3, 1, 'mine-no-and-the-new-self', 'Mine, No, and the New Self', 'draft', $t$A twenty-month-old is standing in front of the hallway mirror with a smear of blueberry on her nose. She looks, frowns, and reaches up to rub her own nose. That afternoon she grips a toy truck and announces mine, and at bath time she plants her feet and says no.

Those three moments tend to arrive in the same stretch of the second year. In this lesson, we'll look at when toddlers first recognize themselves, and at what mine and no really mean. We'll also look at how often toddlers go along with us, which is more often than it feels.

Researchers have a simple way to ask whether a child knows herself. An adult quietly dabs a spot of color on the child's nose, and then the child is placed in front of a mirror.

A child who doesn't yet recognize herself treats the reflection as someone else. In a classic study of eighty-eight children, babies from about six to twelve months greeted the mirror like a friendly playmate.

A child who does recognize herself looks in the mirror and then touches her own nose. That small reach tells us she understands that the child in the glass is her.

So when does that reach appear? One study saw ninety-eight babies every three months through the second year. Recognizing themselves in the mirror emerged between eighteen and twenty-one months, the same stretch in which pretend play appeared in Module 1.

In the classic study, about two thirds of the children between twenty and twenty-four months recognized themselves. That leaves a third who did not yet.

So for most of these children it arrived late in the second year, and for some it came later still.

The mirror test is a window, and it should not be treated as a deadline. When researchers tried it in other parts of the world, the results changed.

One study followed two hundred and seventy-six toddlers in Germany, India, and Cameroon. Recognition grew with age everywhere. It was more common in the city families, where mothers put more weight on a child acting independently, and less common in the rural families.

In another study, children in Kenya rarely reached for the mark at all, even well past two. The researchers questioned whether the test can be read as a universal marker of a sense of self.

Around the same time, other signs of a self show up. Children start to use words like me, my, and I. In a study of sixty-six children, the ones who recognized themselves in the mirror used more of these words, and their pretend play was more advanced.

A new feeling appears as well. In two small studies, toddlers were put in situations meant to bring out either fear or embarrassment. Embarrassment went with recognizing themselves in the mirror, and wariness did not.

Embarrassment needs a self to be embarrassed about. So a toddler who suddenly turns shy when everyone looks at her may be showing a new sense of self.

Knowing yourself also seems to open a door to other people. In a set of experiments with a hundred and twenty-six children, a playmate became upset. Almost all the children who recognized themselves in the mirror showed concern and tried to help. The children who did not yet recognize themselves looked puzzled or carried on.

A more recent study, which followed seventy-two toddlers month by month, was more cautious. Self-recognition came before comforting someone in distress, but it did not seem to cause it directly.

Either way, the toddler who has just discovered me is also beginning to notice you.

To study mine, researchers watched sixty-six British toddlers at home with children they knew, on two visits six months apart. Words like mine and my appeared between eighteen and twenty-four months.

Toddlers who said mine were also more likely to say yours. And although saying mine went with more grabbing and pushing at first, the toddlers who used these words at the first visit were more likely to share with their friends six months later.

So mine is not the opposite of sharing. It is the first half of an idea whose second half is yours.

What looks like selfishness is partly knowledge. In one study, toddlers between eighteen and twenty-eight months already showed a basic understanding of which things were theirs and which were someone else's.

In another, two-year-olds were given toys and told the toys were theirs. When they played with a friend, the owners were more likely to keep hold of their toys and to say mine. But they also pointed out which toys were their friend's, said yours, and handed those toys back.

A toddler who shouts mine is applying a rule about who owns what. She applies it loudly, and mostly in her own favor, but it is a rule she can also apply to other people's things.

All of this matters for sharing. In one study, toddlers of eighteen and twenty-four months had toys or snacks, while an adult playmate had none and made her wish for some more and more obvious.

The two-year-olds shared often, and without much prompting. The eighteen-month-olds shared when they had several chances and the adult spelled out what she wanted. At both ages, the toddlers who understood ownership better shared more, whatever their age or language.

So understanding what is mine seems to come before real sharing. Giving up something of their own was the hardest kind of helping at both eighteen and thirty months, and the Preschool class follows sharing from three to five.

The other famous word is no, and it is one of the first words many children say. Studies of early speech find that early nos tend to be refusals. Using no to say that something isn't true comes later, most often between twenty and twenty-five months in one study.

Think about what a no requires. A child has to notice that what she wants is different from what you want, and then tell you. A baby can cry or turn away, but it takes a child with a sense of me to refuse.

It may be why no and mine tend to arrive in the same season as the reach toward the nose in the mirror.

It is natural to read a toddler's no as a sign that something has gone wrong. One study of one-year-olds and young two-year-olds found something close to the opposite.

The toddlers who resisted their mothers most actively tended to have mothers who were more supportive, who gave them more say, and who had fewer symptoms of depression. These toddlers also started more friendly exchanges with their mothers.

As the children got older, willing cooperation went up, and so did active resistance. Where mothers were struggling with depression, that rise in resistance was missing. The findings fit the idea that pushing back at this age often reflects a child's wish to have a hand in events, and does not point to poor parenting or a troubled relationship.

Refusing also changes shape with age. A study that followed children from the toddler years into preschool found that conflicts between mothers and children peaked at around two and a half.

The findings suggest that toddlers rely mostly on plain refusal, and that preschoolers rely more and more on negotiating.

A separate study watched how these standoffs ended for children from fifteen months to four years. With older children, mothers succeeded right away more often, compromises were more common, and mothers had to enforce things less.

With all this refusing, how often do toddlers go along? It is more often than it feels at six in the evening.

When researchers watched ninety children of twenty-seven months at home with their mothers, they described the children as generally obedient. In day care, children of two, three, and four went along with eighty-four percent of requests on average.

Refusing is just as typical. When parents of forty children without behavior problems recorded refusals at home for two weeks, thirty-seven of the children refused often enough to meet a diagnostic checklist's bar for a symptom.

Much also depends on the moment. In a lab study of toddlers in five situations, cooperation swung widely from one to the next. So there is no standard number of nos per day, because the same child can be easy in one moment and immovable in the next.

Not every yes is the same. The study from Module 1 that compared do requests with don't requests also separated two kinds of cooperation. In one, the child goes along without much heart in it. In the other, the child takes up the request eagerly, as if it were her own idea.

Only the wholehearted kind was linked to following the rule when the child was alone. In a related study, wholehearted cooperation was most common in pairs who shared a lot of enjoyment with each other.

So the road to a child who follows rules on her own seems to run through the relationship more than through winning each standoff. The next lesson turns that into practice.

Some toddlers argue far more than others, and their parents often worry about what that says. A study of sixty-four mothers and toddlers looked at this question.

How often a pair clashed was related to the child's temperament, including how negatively the child reacted to things and how active and impulsive the child was. It was not related to how secure the bond between mother and child was.

What the secure pairs had was a different kind of clash, with more explaining, more compromise, and more disputes that actually got settled. So a high count of nos is tied to temperament, which the Infant class covered. The relationship shows in how the clashes go.

What does this change at home? Start by hearing mine and no as news about development. They are not a verdict on your parenting, and they are not a plan to defeat you.

With mine, say out loud who owns what. This is your truck, and that one is Sam's. When she has to hand something back, name that too, because it is his. You are teaching the second half of the idea.

With no, decide which ones you can simply accept. Letting a no stand when it costs nothing is not giving in. For the limits that matter, you will need warmth and a plan, and that is the next lesson.

Here are three things to take with you.

A self arrives in the second year. In the studies we covered, most toddlers recognized themselves by about two, and me, mine, and no arrive in the same period.

Mine comes before real sharing. Toddlers who understand what is theirs also learn yours, and they tend to share more.

A no is not a failure. Active refusing at this age goes along with supportive parenting, and young children still go along with most requests.

So the child who rubs the blueberry off her own nose, guards her truck, and refuses the bath is a child busy becoming someone. Your job is not to stop that, and it is not to step aside either.

In the next lesson, we'll look at choices and warm limits, the two tools that let a toddler have a say while you stay in charge, and I'll see you in that lesson.$t$),
  ('toddlerhood', 3, 2, 'choices-and-warm-limits', 'Choices and Warm Limits', 'draft', $t$It is time to leave the playground. A father says so, and his two-year-old looks at him, laughs, and runs for the slide. He feels two instincts pulling at him, one to grab her and march to the car, and the other to plead, bargain, and offer a snack.

There is a third way, and it is where the research points. In this lesson, we'll look at what it means to support a toddler's independence, and at choices that work and choices that backfire. We'll see how to hold a limit warmly, why explaining helps a little, and when to stop.

Researchers describe two broad ways of getting a child to do something. One, called controlling, leans on pressure, like threats or anger. The other is called supporting autonomy, which means protecting a child's sense that her actions are her own.

Supporting autonomy does not mean dropping the request. In a study of a hundred and eighty-two parents of two-year-olds, researchers identified a handful of habits used while asking for something dull but necessary. Parents showed they understood how the child felt, gave a reason the child could follow, described the problem without blame, and showed what to do.

The parents who used those habits more had toddlers who had taken the family's rules on board more fully.

The studies of toddlers start at twelve months. In one, forty-one mothers played with their one-year-olds. The babies whose mothers supported their own efforts kept at a toy longer, and handled it more skillfully.

A second study measured the same thing at twelve and at twenty months. Toddlers whose mothers supported their independence were more persistent and more capable in play than toddlers whose mothers were more controlling.

So the first thing this style seems to go with is a toddler's willingness to keep trying.

The more surprising finding is about rules. Researchers watched about a hundred two-year-olds with a parent during two tests of cooperation. One was cleaning up toys, and the other was leaving an attractive toy alone.

They scored how each parent handled it, and then saw the children again at three and a half. Children whose parents had used more supportive strategies at two showed more wholehearted cooperation by three and a half. Children whose parents had used more controlling strategies showed less than before.

Wholehearted cooperation, remember, is the kind that lasts when you leave the room, so pressure may cost more than it gains.

Longer studies point the same way. In one, mothers' support for independence at around fifteen months was the strongest predictor, among the parenting qualities measured, of how well children could hold back an impulse and keep a rule in mind at eighteen months and at two.

A study of more than thirteen hundred children found that mothers' support for independence in the first three years predicted self-control before kindergarten, and school achievement all the way to high school.

Studies like these watch families and can't prove cause. The Preschool class describes an experiment that tested the idea directly with three-year-olds.

None of this means letting a toddler run the house, because having a say is different from being in charge.

In a classic experiment, first and second graders were given rules for a painting activity, either as orders or in an informing way, and a third group had no rules.

The children who got the orders painted with less interest and less creativity. The children who got the same limits in the informing way did as well as the children with no limits. Those children were older than toddlers, but the point carries: a limit can be firm and still respect the child.

One of the simplest ways to give a toddler a say is to offer a choice. A review that combined forty-one studies found that having a choice raised interest, effort, and performance, and the effect on interest was stronger for children than for adults.

In that review, small choices worked best, like which color or which cup, and two to four choices in a row worked better than fewer or more.

Newer studies add toddlers to the picture. In a sticker game, children of about two and a half chose to have a choice on seventy percent of turns, and in child care rooms, two-year-olds stayed with a toy longer when a teacher offered a choice of two than when she handed over one. Whether choosing changes behavior is less settled, since studies of preschoolers choosing vegetables have gone both ways.

For a toddler, a choice that works has three features. It has two options, both options are fine with you, and it sits inside the limit instead of replacing it.

So the limit is that we are leaving the playground, and the choice is whether to walk to the car or ride on your shoulders. The limit is that teeth get brushed, and the choice is the red toothbrush or the blue one.

Then honor what she picks. A choice you overrule teaches her that choosing is pointless, and the Preschool class returns to why real choices matter.

Choices can also go wrong, in three ways. The first is the wide-open choice. Adults in experiments were less likely to pick anything from twenty-four or thirty options than from six, although toddlers in the sticker game showed no such overload. The practical reason to offer two is that both must be answers you can accept.

The second is the false choice, which is a question asked when the answer isn't open. Asking whether she is ready to leave, or adding okay to the end of an instruction, invites a no that you can't accept.

The third is offering a choice about the limit itself, or in the middle of a meltdown. If something has to happen, say that it is happening, and put the choice somewhere smaller.

Toddlers need limits, as Module 1 said, and the question is how to hold one. The short answer from the research is to be warm and firm at the same time, which this class calls a warm limit.

A review of forty-one studies of preschoolers found that the way parents steer was linked to how well children cooperate, though the links were small. Toddler studies show the direction. Among thirty-month-olds, children whose mothers guided more and forced less cooperated more wholeheartedly.

In a study of two hundred and fifty-six families, insistent control at eighteen months went with less wholehearted cooperation two years later.

A warm limit has two parts. You name the feeling, and you hold the limit. You are sad to leave the swings, and it is time to go.

The first part is not a trick to get your way. It tells your child that her feeling makes sense, even though it doesn't change the answer.

The two common slips each drop one half. One is naming the feeling and then giving up the limit. The other is holding the limit while brushing the feeling aside, which Module 1 showed tends to make the upset stronger. Accepting a feeling while holding a limit means keeping both.

Holding a limit with a toddler usually means acting as well as talking. Say what you are going to do, and then do it calmly. I'm going to pick you up now, and we'll walk to the car.

In one experiment, mothers were on the phone while their toddlers kept demanding attention. Some mothers were asked to reprimand every time. Others were asked to reprimand half the time, and to give warm attention the other half.

The toddlers who got the mix demanded more, and were more upset. Giving in some of the time seemed to teach them to keep pushing, so the same answer each time works better.

Should you explain your limits to a toddler? The evidence says a little. In a study of sixty-three mothers and toddlers, researchers recorded their disagreements at two and a half.

Some mothers gave reasons, softened their position where they could, and brought disagreements to a real ending. Six months later, their children scored higher on a set of measures that included understanding feelings and early conscience.

A second study found that reasoning with older toddlers went with better social skills later, while limits and prohibitions alone showed no such link. So giving reasons goes with better understanding later, even on days when it changes nothing in the moment.

The trouble starts when explaining is the whole plan. A review written for children's doctors looked at studies of reasoning with toddlers and preschoolers. In most circumstances, words alone did not change young children's problem behavior.

Another study followed two- and three-year-olds. Disruptive behavior rose most where parents reasoned often but never backed it up with a consequence. It fell most where parents reasoned often and did back it up when they needed to.

One research measure of discipline even counts long-winded talking as a common mistake. So give one short reason, once, and then act. In a full meltdown even that is too much, as Module 4 will show.

The easiest limit to hold is the one you never need. In a study of twenty-four mothers shopping with their two-and-a-half-year-olds, every mother used two kinds of tactics. Some were reactions to trouble, and others came first, by steering the child's attention elsewhere.

The mothers who used more of the second kind had children who did fewer of the things their mothers didn't want. A study of three-year-olds found the same split, and the children of mothers who mostly reacted had more behavior problems two years later.

So think ahead to the hard spots in your day, and fill them before they fill themselves. Module 1 covered distraction and changing the room, and Module 4 covers routines.

Let's go back to the playground and put it together. Five minutes before leaving, the father gives a heads-up, and then tells his daughter that this is the last slide.

When it is time, he gets close and says it plainly. It's time to go home for dinner, and you wish we could stay. Do you want to walk or ride on my shoulders? She runs anyway, so he tells her he is going to pick her up, and he does it without anger.

She may cry all the way to the car, and that doesn't mean it failed. He named the feeling, held the limit, offered a real choice, gave one reason, and followed through. Repeated over months, with your help, that is how limits get learned.

Here are three things to take with you.

Give a say, and keep the limit. Supporting a toddler's independence goes with more trying now and more willing cooperation later.

Offer two real choices inside the limit. Skip the false question, and honor what she picks.

Name the feeling, hold the limit. Give one short reason, say what you will do, and then do it calmly.

So a toddler can have a say without being in charge, and you can stay in charge without a fight over everything. Warm limits take longer to say than orders, and they ask more of you, but they are where the research points.

In the next lesson, we'll look at the toddler's drive to do things by herself, and at how to turn it into real help around the house, and I'll see you in that lesson.$t$),
  ('toddlerhood', 3, 3, 'i-do-it-turning-independence-into-help', 'I Do It: Turning Independence Into Help', 'draft', $t$A mother is unloading the dishwasher in a hurry when her twenty-two-month-old arrives, announces me do it, and takes over the spoons. He carries them to the drawer one at a time, and a job that takes her two minutes is going to take ten.

In this lesson, we'll look at where that drive comes from and what it is for. We'll see how early children join in household work, and what parents do that brings them in. We'll finish with how to slow down enough to let a toddler help, and what to do when you can't.

Researchers have a plain name for this, which is wanting to do it oneself. In one study, forty-one children from nine months to six years played with a set of tasks. Every so often, the adult interrupted what the child was doing.

The children's reactions fell into stages, and insisting on doing it themselves went along with how far their sense of self had developed.

So me do it belongs with mine and no from the first lesson of this module. It is the new self asking to be the one who makes things happen.

What toddlers want from a task also changes. Researchers watched children from fifteen months to almost three during play and cleanup tasks.

The youngest were mostly interested in the activity itself. Older toddlers increasingly aimed at an end result. They checked their work, fixed mistakes, and showed pleasure when it came out right.

A second study, of fifty-seven toddlers, found that the older ones were more insistent on doing the very last step of a task themselves. If you have ever been told off for pressing the elevator button, you have met this finding.

Somewhere near the second birthday, pride shows up. In one small study of twelve toddlers and their mothers, proud reactions to succeeding became common by about twenty months.

A larger set of studies found a shift after twenty-one months. Older toddlers looked up at the adult after doing something themselves, more than after watching the adult do the same thing. They also called their mothers over to see what they had done.

In those studies, praise added to children's pleasure in succeeding, but only modestly.

Persistence can be picked up by watching. In a set of experiments with two hundred and sixty-two fifteen-month-olds, some babies watched an adult struggle to reach a goal, trying different ways before succeeding. Others watched the adult succeed at once.

Then each baby was given a difficult new task. The babies who had seen the adult struggle made more attempts.

As Module 1 put it, your toddler is studying you. So let her see you try, fail, and try again.

The opposite also seems to be true. In experiments with four- and five-year-olds, an adult either took over a task, taught the child how to do it, or did neither. The children whose task was taken over persisted less on the next one.

Those children were older, but a study of two-year-olds points the same way. Mothers taught their toddlers a hard task, and researchers scored how they did it. A year later, the children of mothers who had supported their child's own efforts were less likely to avoid challenges.

Children whose mothers had criticized them showed more shame at three, and children who had been corrected and encouraged kept at things longer. So stepping in too fast may have a cost, even when it is kindly meant.

Now put that drive next to a basket of laundry. In a classic study, researchers set up a room like a home and asked parents and other adults to do ordinary chores there. Nobody asked the children to do anything.

Children of eighteen, twenty-four, and thirty months joined in with most of the chores, promptly and on their own. They talked about what they were doing, they showed that they understood the goals, and they even added fitting steps that no adult had shown them.

Experiments with children as young as fourteen to eighteen months show a readiness to help a stranger with a simple problem, and the Preschool class tells that story in full.

Why do they do it? One study followed toddlers at eighteen, twenty-one, and twenty-four months through a shared chore. Partway through, the adult was interrupted and stopped working.

At eighteen months, toddlers were less likely to keep going once the adult had stopped. At twenty-one and twenty-four months, they were more likely to carry on by themselves.

The researchers' reading is that toddlers begin by helping because they want to be part of what you are doing. Over the following months, that grows into helping out of concern and a sense of responsibility. On this reading, the company comes first, and the sense of contributing follows.

Toddler help is real, and it is also often not helpful. A survey of more than five hundred families with children from one to four asked about nine common chores.

Most parents reported that their child's help was sometimes unhelpful. The researchers describe young children joining in cheerfully, including in ways that make the chore harder, and they titled their paper happily unhelpful.

That is worth knowing in advance. Imperfect helping is what most parents report at this age, and you can expect it without reading it as a failure of the plan.

How do parents bring toddlers in? In one study, forty-six parents did a household chore with their toddler. With eighteen-month-olds, parents gave small concrete requests, like put this one in the basket, and kept guiding the child's attention.

With two-year-olds, parents talked more about what was needed and about the child's part as a helper, and they voiced more approval. So parents used different approaches at the two ages.

An experiment shows that encouragement matters early on. Among babies under fifteen months, those who were encouraged and praised helped twice as often as those who weren't, and they kept helping afterward. Older babies were not affected by it.

Toddlers help in very different cultures, and families ask for that help in different ways. One study compared families in rural Brazil, in a Brazilian city, and in a German city, with children from eighteen to thirty months.

Brazilian mothers tended to be serious and insistent, and German mothers tended to ask, explain, and coax. In the rural Brazilian families and in the German families, the local way of asking went with more helping.

In another study, eighteen-month-olds in Delhi helped more than eighteen-month-olds in a German city. The Delhi mothers reported giving their toddlers more chances to help around the home, and praising them less for it. And in interviews in the United States, Mexican-heritage and European American mothers both said that most of their two- and three-year-olds helped on their own initiative.

What about rewards for helping? The Preschool class covers that research, and the short version is that giving a toddler a prize for helping made helping less likely later.

Toddlers don't seem to need one. In a study of Dutch and Chinese toddlers, children looked happier after helping an adult than they had before, and being thanked did not change that. The helping itself seemed to be rewarding.

So a warm thank you is plenty. Tell him what the help did, for example that now the spoons are all put away, and leave the prizes out of it.

What can a toddler actually do? Think of real jobs with a clear ending, cut down to one small piece. A one-year-old can drop socks into the laundry basket, put a spoon in the drawer, or carry a clean diaper to you.

A two-year-old can wipe a spill with a cloth, pour a scoop of food into the dog's bowl, put shoes by the door, or water one plant. Looking after himself counts too. By eighteen months, most children help with dressing by pushing an arm through a sleeve, and hold out their hands to be washed.

Choose jobs that truly need doing, because finishing something real is what he is after.

The hard part is time. A toddler's help is slow, and most family mornings are not. So choose your moments, and build the extra minutes into one or two parts of the day where you can afford them. It helps to see the slow version as practice, because when researchers described getting dressed to parents of young children as a chance to learn, the parents took over far less, by nearly half.

Give him one piece of the job, and make it the last piece when you can. You load the washing machine, and he pushes the door shut. Remember that older toddlers are especially keen to do the final step themselves.

Then wait a little longer than is comfortable before you step in. If he is stuck, help with the smallest thing that gets him moving again, and hand the job back.

Things will spill, and that is part of the arrangement. When the water goes on the floor, say what happened and what comes next. The water spilled, so let's get the cloth.

Keep your words about the job, and not about what kind of child he is. Research with older children, which the Preschool class describes, suggests that being called a helper can make a mishap feel worse.

And try not to redo his work in front of him. If the towels have to be refolded, do it later, so that what he takes from the moment is that his work counted.

Sometimes me do it can't happen, because the stove is hot or the bus leaves in four minutes. This is a job for the warm limit from the last lesson.

Name the wish, hold the limit, and offer a part. You want to do the buckle yourself. Today I'm doing the buckle, and you can pull the strap tight. Tomorrow we'll leave time for you to try.

Then keep that promise when you can. The message to protect is that his wish to do things is welcome in your home, even on the days when there is no time for it.

Here are three things to take with you.

Me do it is the self at work. Toddlers want to make things happen and to finish them, and pride follows from about twenty months.

Toddlers are helpers in the family. They join real work on their own, and their help is often imperfect at first.

Slow down and hand over a piece. Give a real job with a clear end, wait before stepping in, and thank him without prizes.

That brings us to the end of Module 3. A toddler with a self says mine and no, needs choices and warm limits, and wants to do things alongside you. All three are the same growing independence, and all three go better when you make room for it.

In the next lesson, we'll begin Module 4 with tantrums, looking at what they are, how common they are, and what is typical, and I'll see you in that lesson.$t$),
  ('toddlerhood', 4, 1, 'inside-a-tantrum', 'Inside a Tantrum', 'draft', $t$A twenty-month-old in a supermarket wants to hold the bananas. Her father says not right now, and within seconds she is on the floor by the cereal, screaming, with her legs kicking and a dozen strangers pretending not to look.

This module is about those minutes, and the ones around them. In this lesson, we'll look at what a tantrum is, how common and how long tantrums are at this age, and the shape they take from start to finish. Then we'll look at what is typical, and at the signs worth a conversation with your child's doctor.

Module 1 gave us a fast alarm and slow brakes. A tantrum is what it looks like when the alarm is at full volume and the brakes have let go altogether.

Researchers describe a tantrum as a sudden, intense burst of negative feeling out of proportion to what set it off. The word matters less than the picture, in which a wish is blocked, the body floods, and for a few minutes the toddler is not choosing anything.

Hold that framing through this module. Nothing in a tantrum is a decision, and the brakes that would make it one are, as Lesson 1.2 showed, years from finished.

Nearly every toddler has them. A review puts the figure at up to eighty-five percent of young children in countries like ours, and the Preschool class describes a study in which more than eight in ten of fifteen hundred preschoolers had tantrums sometimes.

In a Finnish survey of a hundred and thirty-two children, nearly nine in ten had tantrums, about two thirds began at two or three, and more than half had stopped by five.

A Belgian survey of more than seven hundred children found tantrums one to six times a week, lasting two to four minutes, and if that sounds like your house, your house is ordinary.

Tantrums become common at around eighteen months, which is no accident, because it is when walking, wanting, and the word no have all arrived, and the brakes have not.

In a Dutch study of eight hundred and sixty-one children from one to five, tantrums grew less frequent with age, but the ones that happened tended to last longer. Older children have fewer, longer storms.

By five or six, fewer than half of children are still having tantrums at all. The decline is slow and uneven, and it comes from the brakes growing, not from anything you say during the storm.

How long is a tantrum? The answer is shorter than it feels. In the largest study of its kind, parents of three hundred and thirty-five children from eighteen months to five wrote accounts of tantrums as they happened.

The most common length was between half a minute and one minute. Three quarters of all tantrums were over within five minutes.

Even when clinicians timed three hundred and thirty tantrums in children with serious behavior problems, the average was about three and a half minutes. Memory stretches them, so a tantrum that seems to go on forever has usually been a few minutes.

Those detailed accounts also revealed a shape. Researchers coded what the children did in each half minute, and found two separate processes running at once, which they called anger and distress.

Anger rises fast, peaks at or near the start, and then falls away, and screaming, stamping, kicking, and throwing belong to it. Distress rises slowly through the tantrum, and whining, crying, and reaching for comfort belong to that.

Recordings of children's voices confirm the two groups, with screaming and yelling in one family of sounds and crying, whining, and fussing in another. A tantrum is anger with sadness underneath it, and the sadness outlasts the anger.

That shape is the most useful thing in this lesson. The first minute is anger, and almost nothing reaches a child in it. The last minutes are sadness, and a child in them is usually looking for you.

In the study of three hundred and thirty-five children, tantrums in which the child dropped to the floor in the first half minute were shorter, and parents stepped in less. The storm often burns out faster when it is allowed to peak.

In a study of seventy-five Chinese toddlers whose mothers took a toy away, anger often began at low intensity and only sometimes escalated. Lesson 4.3 is about what to do with all of this, and for now, notice that the ending of a tantrum is a reach for comfort.

Many tantrums include hitting, biting, or kicking, and these are typical too. Among five hundred and eleven seventeen-month-olds in a population study, close to eighty percent had already hit, bitten, or kicked someone.

In a survey of nearly five hundred American children under two, ninety-four percent had done something physically aggressive in the past month, and a Norwegian study of more than a thousand children found that hitting peaks at about twenty to twenty-two months.

The Preschool class covers the decline from the third birthday on. For now, a one-year-old who hits is doing what one-year-olds do, and learning not to hit is the work of the next few years.

Some toddlers hit or bang their own heads in a tantrum, which frightens parents more than anything else. In the Dutch study, about two in five children fell into a profile with both aggression and self-hitting, so it is not rare.

Rhythmic head banging in a crib is a different thing, found in older surveys in four to fifteen percent of typically developing children. It starts before twelve months, happens when a child is drowsy or alone, and fades by three.

Either kind is distressing to watch, and neither usually causes injury beyond a bruise. We'll come back to where self-hurting fits among the signs worth checking.

What starts a tantrum is something parents agree on. In a survey of nearly three hundred parents of one- to three-year-olds, nine in ten named a refused request, and as many named having to start, stop, or change an activity.

In the clinic study that timed tantrums, half began with a child being asked to do something.

So the common triggers are a blocked wish and a transition. Lesson 4.2 takes up transitions in full, because they are the more preventable of the two.

Language matters more than most parents expect. Among two thousand and one mothers of children from twelve to thirty-eight months, toddlers with fewer spoken words had more frequent and more out-of-control tantrums.

Late talkers at two to two and a half were nearly twice as likely to have severe tantrums as children whose language was typical, and the link was already there in the second year.

Among a hundred and twenty children followed from eighteen months to four, those whose language grew fastest looked least angry by four, partly because they could ask for help and look elsewhere. Words are brakes in the making, which is one more reason Lesson 2.3 matters.

Some toddlers have more tantrums than others, and Lesson 1.2 covered much of why. Children who react strongly from the start, who are worn down, or who are bothered by noise and texture reach their limit sooner.

In the Belgian survey, more intense toddlers had more frequent tantrums six months later, and a study that checked on three hundred and thirty-three toddlers every two months from their first birthday found each child's irritability fairly steady over the year.

So a toddler who melts down often at fourteen months will probably still do so at two, which does not mean something is wrong. It means you know your child, and can plan for her.

Put the pieces together and a typical picture appears. Most toddlers have tantrums, several times a week, lasting a few minutes, starting around eighteen months and peaking in the second and third years, with hitting mixed in.

Researchers who study the line between typical and worrying describe the typical tantrum as one that happens in an expectable situation, such as being told no, being tired, or having to leave.

The tantrums that concerned them came out of nowhere, went on and on, or broke things. Context, length, and destructiveness mattered more than the simple count.

The Preschool class gives four signs for three- to five-year-olds, behavior that is frequent, intense, lasting, and everywhere. For toddlers, researchers have looked for something similar.

Among three hundred and fifty fourteen-month-olds, five things best picked out the toddlers whose irritability was getting in the way at home. They were frustrated by small things, hit, bit, or kicked in tantrums, could not cheer up when grumpy, were grumpy during fun, and had tantrums in public.

Frequent on its own is common, and in a Quebec birth cohort about one in eight seventeen-month-olds already showed frequent defiant behavior. When a version of that screen was tried at two-year checkups, a quarter of toddlers scored high, far more than will ever have a problem, so a high score is a reason to look again, not a diagnosis.

Looking again is the key. Among more than four hundred preschoolers followed for several years, a quarter were highly irritable at the start, but only two thirds of those stayed that way, and fewer than one in a hundred who started low became high.

Quality predicted lasting trouble more than count. Among two hundred and seventy-nine preschoolers, children with behavior disorders were violent in tantrums more often, had them at school, and recovered more slowly, and depressed children were more likely to hurt themselves.

In the Dutch study, frequent tantrums went with later acting-out problems, long tantrums with later worry and withdrawal, and the aggressive, self-hurting profile predicted both. From preschool to school age, hitting, biting, or kicking in tantrums and trouble recovering from anger were the two behaviors that most predicted wider trouble.

So here is the short list. Ask your child's doctor when tantrums happen many times a day for weeks, regularly last more than fifteen or twenty minutes, usually involve hurting himself or others, leave him unable to recover, or happen everywhere and with everyone.

Add language to the list, because a toddler with few words and severe tantrums deserves the hearing and language check from Lesson 2.3. In one comparison, autism did not make preschoolers' tantrums more common, but it did make them more likely to come without a clear trigger or over a failure to communicate.

And notice your own load. Parents of children with problematic tantrums carry a measurably heavier burden, and if the tantrums are wearing you down, that counts as a reason to ask.

Here are three things to take with you.

Tantrums are typical and short. Most toddlers have several a week, most are over within five minutes, and they peak around two and fade by five.

A tantrum has a shape. Anger comes first and burns out fast, and sadness follows and reaches for you.

Quality matters more than count. Tantrums that come from nowhere, last very long, hurt someone, or leave a child unable to recover are the ones to raise with your doctor.

A tantrum is the alarm at full volume with the brakes still being built. It is not a verdict on your child or on you, and most will fade as the brakes grow.

In the next lesson, we'll look at the transitions and routines of a toddler's day, where most of the preventable tantrums begin, and I'll see you in that lesson.$t$),
  ('toddlerhood', 4, 2, 'heading-it-off-routines-and-transitions', 'Heading It Off: Routines and Transitions', 'draft', $t$It is five o'clock at the park, and a two-year-old has been told it is time to go home. He was happy a moment ago. Now he is running the other way, and when his mother catches up, the bucket goes flying and the screaming starts.

Lesson 4.1 showed that a refused wish and a change of activity are the two most common triggers, and this lesson is about the second. We'll look at why transitions set off meltdowns, what warnings and routines can and can't do, the daycare goodbye, and the pile-up before the storm.

A toddler's day is a chain of endings. Getting dressed, leaving the house, snack, play, lunch, nap, pickup, dinner, bath, and bed are each an ending as well as a beginning.

Researchers who watch early childhood classrooms count fifteen to twenty transitions a day, and in one study more than a third of the time observed was spent moving from one thing to the next.

Transitions are also where the trouble clusters. Among a hundred and forty-five preschoolers, children had more conflict during routines and transitions than in any other part of the day.

Why is stopping so much harder than starting? Two reasons stand out in the research. The first is loss, because leaving the park means giving up something good for something not yet real.

The second is unpredictability, since an ending from nowhere is one the child could not prepare for. Module 1 showed that a toddler can know a rule without being able to follow it, and that the brakes that would let her pause and switch are still being built.

So a two-year-old asked to stop mid-sandcastle must let go of a goal, hold a new one in mind, and manage the feeling in between, with equipment years from ready. The alarm from Lesson 1.2 fires over the loss, and the tantrum follows.

The classic advice is to give a warning, and the research on warnings is small and more mixed than the advice suggests. In one study, forty preschoolers moved on more smoothly when given verbal warnings before an activity change.

Two other small studies found the opposite. A two-minute warning given to toddlers in child care did nothing on its own, and neither did a toy to carry through the transition or advance notice given to three noncompliant preschoolers.

What worked in those studies was the warning plus calm follow-through, the adult saying what was coming and then making it happen without a fuss. That is the warm limit from Lesson 3.2, and why a warning is a beginning rather than a solution.

A toddler's preview is short, concrete, and close to the event. Tell her at the gate what will happen at the end, such as after the slide and the swings we go home for dinner, then give one warning a minute or two before in the same words, and name the last turn as the last turn.

Pictures help some families through transitions. The Preschool class describes picture sequences for three- to five-year-olds, and the toddler research is thinner, since picture schedules have mostly been tested with children who have autism or behavior problems, and the pictures alone did little, so a few photos by the door are a habit to try rather than a finding.

The best transitions give the toddler a job. Let him do the last step, which Lesson 3.3 showed older toddlers want anyway. He puts the bucket in the bag and carries it to the gate.

Give him something to carry, something to look for, or a song that belongs to leaving. In one study, transitions in which adults talked, praised, and played along produced fewer rule-breaking episodes than silent ones.

None of this stops every tantrum, and all of it lowers the count.

A routine is the slow version of the same idea, a transition that happens the same way every time, so that the ending is known before it arrives.

A review of a hundred and seventy studies found routines linked with better self-regulation, social skills, and health, most strongly in families under strain. In a British study of nearly eleven thousand three-year-olds, children with regular bedtimes and mealtimes had better emotional self-control.

Among nearly three thousand American low-income families, a consistent bedtime routine from fourteen to thirty-six months predicted better emotion regulation at three and fewer problems at ten. Lesson 1.2 gave the Chicago study, the Preschool class takes routines at three to five from here, and none of these patterns can prove that the routine itself does the work.

Why would routines help a toddler? One answer comes from newer research on predictability itself, which times the moment-to-moment pattern of a parent's signals to a baby and measures instability in a household.

Across three groups of children in two countries, unpredictable parental signals early in life went with more fearfulness later, and in an American cohort of nearly five thousand children, unpredictability from one to nine predicted anxiety and behavior problems at fifteen.

None of these studies tested previews or routines, so the link is indirect, but they show that a young brain does better when the world around it has a pattern, as Lesson 1.2 said of disorganized homes.

A toddler routine is a sequence rather than a schedule, so the clock can move while the order stays. Bath, pajamas, two books, song, and bed, in that order, on most nights.

Anchor the day with a few fixed points, such as meals, nap, and bed, and let the rest flex around them. Say the sequence out loud as you go, because hearing it is how a toddler learns to expect it.

Keep routines few and short, because a toddler cannot hold a long sequence, and a routine that takes forty minutes is one you will skip. Expect them to carry less weight when your child is ill or the day is upside down.

Drop-off is the hardest transition of the day for many families, and the Infant class covered how parents leave. Parents of toddlers hovered and snuck out the most, long explanations and lingering went with more crying, and a repeated goodbye ritual went with less stress.

Two more studies add to that. In a German day care, one- to three-year-olds whose parents took a long time to leave played less in the first half hour afterward, and in an American preschool, the longer the parent lingered, the less the child settled.

Toddlers were the most distressed age group at drop-off, and by the second half of the year they protested less and their parents left faster, so the goodbye gets easier with practice.

Starting child care is a transition of its own. In a Norwegian study of a hundred and thirteen toddlers, stress hormone levels were low while a parent was present, rose during the first weeks apart, and were back down by three months, when the children were doing well.

In a German study of seventy fifteen-month-olds, stress hormone levels in the first days without the mother were up to double those at home, and children whose mothers spent more days settling them in stayed securely attached.

So plan for a slow start where you can, and a softer evening after. Keep the goodbye short, with the coat on its hook, one wave at the window, a sentence about coming back after nap, a handover to the same adult, and out the door with a warm face.

Most tantrums at transitions have help, because the child was already tired, or hungry, or had been holding it together in a noisy room all afternoon, and the transition was the last straw.

Lesson 1.2 covered the evidence, including the nap experiments and the way sensitive children reach their limit sooner, and toddlers new to daycare are worn out by evening in the first weeks.

Parents call this the pile-up. Hungry, tired, overstimulated, and lonely for you stack up, and a request that was fine at ten in the morning sets off a storm at five. The skill is to see the stack before the storm.

The stack comes with warning signs. Lesson 4.1 described how anger often begins at low intensity, and whining, clinging, repeated demands, flopping, speeding up, or going quiet are a toddler's low-intensity anger.

In that window, a preview, a snack, a change of room, or a few minutes on your lap can change the direction of the hour. Lesson 1.2 and Lesson 3.2 showed that distraction and steering attention before trouble beat reacting to it.

Once the alarm has fired, the window is closed and the next lesson takes over.

Some weeks the routine is gone, because a trip, an illness, visitors, a move, or a new baby has turned the day upside down, and the number of tantrums goes up. The Family Systems class covers new siblings and family change.

Among more than two thousand children from three to five, those whose routines fell away over two years had more attention, behavior, and emotional problems than those whose routines held.

When the day is off, shrink it. Keep two anchors, usually meals and bed, lower the demands, and expect more meltdowns without reading them as a step backward. The routine is still there to return to.

Prevention lowers the count without taking it to zero, and parents who do everything in this lesson will still have a toddler who melts down several times a week.

Those tantrums come from a brain whose brakes are not finished, and they fade as the brakes grow. A warning without follow-through did nothing in the studies, and nor does a routine that is announced but not kept.

So the goal is fewer and shorter storms, and a toddler who knows what comes next most of the time. When a storm comes anyway, you have not failed, you have arrived at Lesson 4.3.

Go back to the park at five o'clock. At the gate on the way in, his mother says that after the slide and the swings they will go home for dinner, and ten minutes before leaving she says it again and names the last two pushes.

He puts the bucket in the bag himself, because that is his job, and carries it to the gate while they sing the leaving song. He is tired and hungry, so there is a banana in the car.

He may still cry at the gate, but he will probably cry less and for a shorter time, and tomorrow the sequence will be more familiar. That is what prevention looks like with a toddler, a pattern rather than a trick.

Here are three things to take with you.

Transitions are losses, and previews soften them. Tell your toddler what comes next, give one warning, and then follow through calmly.

Routines are endings that are known in advance. Keep a few short sequences and a few fixed anchors, and say them out loud.

Watch for the pile-up before the storm. Tired, hungry, and overloaded come before most meltdowns, and the early signs are your chance to act.

Most of a toddler's preventable tantrums begin at the moments of change in a day, and most of the prevention is a known sequence and a warm, calm ending.

In the next lesson, we'll be inside the storm itself, looking at what helps and what backfires at the peak of a meltdown, and at how to keep your own calm, and I'll see you in that lesson.$t$),
  ('toddlerhood', 4, 3, 'in-the-moment-your-calm-is-their-calm', 'In the Moment: Your Calm Is Their Calm', 'draft', $t$A two-year-old has refused her shoes for the third time, and now she is on the hall floor, red-faced and screaming, while the clock says you should have left five minutes ago. Everything in you wants to make it stop.

This lesson is about those minutes at the peak, what it means to lend a toddler your calm, what helps and what backfires, how to keep your own calm when your alarm is ringing too, and what to do when you can't.

The Infant class introduced the borrowed nervous system, and Lesson 1.2 promised that once the alarm is ringing, a toddler needs to borrow your calm before anything else. That promise comes due here.

Researchers call it co-regulation, an adult helping a child manage feelings the child cannot yet manage alone. The Preschool class describes that job passing to the child between three and five, and at one and two almost none of it has passed.

So in a meltdown, you are the brakes. Hers are not going to arrive in the next three minutes, and the only question is whether the adult in the room is steadier than the child.

Your calm is their calm is a slogan with a body behind it. Across thirty-six studies of parents and children under three in distressing situations, their stress hormone levels rose and fell together, with moderate to strong links.

The reviews add a caution, which is that two bodies in sync is not always good, because a parent whose alarm matches the child's is also in sync. What helps is the steadier body, and the useful direction is yours toward hers.

The clearest evidence comes from the waiting studies. Lesson 1.2 described a hundred and thirty-four two-year-olds waiting while their mothers were busy, and in that study a mother's good mood softened her toddler's next burst of distress, while her bad mood undercut whatever coping the toddler had just managed.

Mothers who brushed the distress aside, held the child back, or offered fewer distractions had toddlers whose distress grew in the next few seconds, and among ninety-six two-year-olds in a frustrating wait, the parents' own ways of handling feelings predicted the toddler's negative feeling.

None of this proves that the parent causes the child's state, since toddlers shape parents too, but it all points one way.

The most useful single study put a hundred and fifty-eight children from fifteen months to three through a frustrating task, once with each parent, and about one in five reached a full tantrum.

The researchers timed what each parent did against what the child did next. When parents distracted the child or reframed the task as a game, frustration fell by about sixty percent with mothers and eighty with fathers, while showing the child how to do it helped less.

Children's own self-comforting cut frustration by a fifth, turning their attention elsewhere cut it by half in the younger children, and pretend play by seventy percent in the older ones, and securely attached children got frustrated later and recovered sooner. Distraction is the first tool, and a child who is sure of you has a head start.

Distraction keeps appearing in the research. Among eighty-two toddlers around eighteen months whose toy was taken away, distraction and gentle redirection reduced upset fastest.

Among fifty-eight thirty-month-olds told not to touch a toy, the mothers of children who managed it used more distraction, and used it before the child reached rather than after.

The catch is timing, which Lesson 4.2 covered. Distraction works on the way up, in the grumbling before the alarm fires, and once a child is at the peak her attention is not available to be steered, so the tool changes.

Lesson 4.1 gave the shape, with anger peaking in the first minute and distress rising slowly toward a reach for comfort, and the tantrums in which a child dropped to the floor early were shorter.

So the first job at the peak is safety, and the second is to wait for the turn, the moment the screaming becomes crying and the child looks for you or lifts her arms, because that is when a toddler can take comfort in.

Until the turn, stay near and stay quiet. You are not ignoring her. You are keeping the room safe and yourself steady, so that when she turns toward you, there is a calm body to turn to.

Here is an honest line about words. No study has timed tantrums against how much the adult says, so the advice to use fewer words rests on what we know about the brakes rather than on a trial.

Lesson 3.2 showed that words alone rarely changed a toddler's behavior, and Lesson 1.2 promised that reasoning in a meltdown rarely works. Among a hundred and two mothers and toddlers, reasoning was the least effective response in the moment to defiance and hitting, and offering an alternative was the most effective response to every kind of refusal.

So at the peak, one short sentence is enough, said once or twice in a low voice, telling her you are here and what will happen when she is ready. Questions, choices, and explanations can wait, as Lesson 3.2 warned.

Does holding a toddler help? Gentle touch lowers young children's stress responses in the studies we have. Among a hundred and fourteen mothers and three-year-olds, more warm touch went with a smaller stress hormone response to a later challenge.

In an experiment with sixty-eight children from three to six, slow stroking of the arm raised the heart's calming rhythm, most of all in children who found self-control hardest.

But none of these tested a hug at the peak of a tantrum, and in the frustration experiment, soothing from mothers made some children more frustrated. Some toddlers at the peak push every hand away, so offer nearness, keep your hands open, and let her choose the moment, which usually comes at the turn.

Some tantrums include hitting, kicking, throwing, or head banging, which Lesson 4.1 showed are common. Safety comes first and needs no words, so move the hard things, put a cushion where the head is going, and block a swinging arm rather than grabbing it.

Hold a child only to stop harm, and let go as soon as the danger has passed, because in the waiting study physical restraint went with more distress, and in a study of ninety-four toddlers more forceful parenting predicted worse tantrums a year later.

In public, the audience is the hardest part, and the steps are the same, so carry her to the car or a quiet corner if you can and wait for the turn there. Siblings belong to the Family Systems class, and the short version is to keep them out of reach.

The study of ninety-four toddlers from twenty to forty-three months is the best evidence on what makes tantrums worse, with tantrums filmed during a cleanup task three times over a year and mothers asked about their discipline.

More power assertion, meaning force, threats, and shouting, predicted tantrums that grew longer and more aggressive over the year, and so did less consistency. Ignoring predicted no change, and the children's tantrums did not change how their mothers parented, so the direction ran from parent to child.

A large review of parenting research found harsh control the strongest companion of acting-out problems. Threats and shouting add a second alarm to a room that already has one.

What about giving in to end it? The common claim is that handing over the thing teaches a toddler that screaming works. No toddler study has tested that directly, and in a daily-diary study of thirty-month-olds, giving in during a single episode did not predict worse behavior.

What predicted fewer problems was consistency across episodes, meaning that most of the time the limit held. So one hard day at the supermarket is not the problem, the pattern over months is, and a limit you now think was wrong can be changed before the storm or well after it, as Lesson 3.2 advised.

Reasoning at the peak is the other common backfire, not harmful so much as useless, and it keeps you talking when quiet would serve, so save the explanation for later, when it can land.

Planned ignoring is common advice, and the evidence is lukewarm, since in the ninety-four toddler study ignoring predicted neither better nor worse tantrums a year later.

The distinction that matters is between ignoring the behavior and ignoring the child. Staying nearby and quiet while the screaming runs its course is the first, and leaving the room or turning your back is the second, which toddlers often escalate against to be seen.

So ignore the show, not the child, and when the anger turns to crying, move in, because ignoring that stage is ignoring a reach for you.

The hardest part is that your alarm is ringing too. Across fifty-five studies of parents during their young child's distress, parents' stress systems responded, and stronger reactions went with less sensitive responses, especially in frustrating tasks.

Your thoughts fuel the alarm too, since parents who judged a child's emotion as unjustified felt more anger, and anger went with harsher responses, so the thought that he is doing this on purpose is itself a trigger.

The practical move is small. Breathe out slowly, drop your shoulders, lower your voice, and tell yourself what Lesson 1.2 taught, that this is an alarm with no brakes, not a choice. Then say your one sentence, because regulating yourself first is the only order that works.

Can parents get better at this? Yes, and there is a toddler trial, in which three hundred parents of eighteen- to thirty-six-month-olds were randomly assigned to a six-session program or a comparison group.

A year later, the parents who took it dismissed feelings less, coached more, and reported fewer struggles with their own emotions, and their toddlers' behavior improved and their stress hormone, measured in hair, had fallen, which did not happen in the comparison group. The Preschool class describes the same program for four- and five-year-olds.

Programs built around mindfulness raise parents' mindfulness, though their effects on children are less clear. Staying steady is a skill, not a temperament.

Sometimes you simply can't do it. The Infant class gave a plan for a crying baby that holds for a toddler. If she is safe, step out of the room for a minute, breathe, and come back when you are steadier, or hand over to another adult.

Stepping away is not leaving her alone in distress for long, and it is not failure, because what mattered in the ninety-four toddler study was the pattern over a year, not any one episode.

And if you are at the end of your rope most days, that is information about your load rather than your character, and the Family Systems class takes up parental stress. The next lesson is about afterward, including after the times you shouted.

Here are three things to take with you.

You are the brakes for now. Steady your own body first, because your state reaches your child's body whether you speak or not.

Distract before the peak, and wait at it. Steer attention while the anger is low, then keep her safe and stay quiet until the screaming turns to crying.

Force and inconsistency make tantrums worse over time. Ignoring the show did neither harm nor good, and reasoning can wait for later.

At the peak of a meltdown, the goal is not to win or to teach. It is to be the calm body in the room until your child can borrow it.

In the next lesson, we'll look at what happens after the storm, reconnecting, repairing after you lost your temper, and talking about it later, and then I'll close the class, and I'll see you there.$t$),
  ('toddlerhood', 4, 4, 'after-the-storm-repair', 'After the Storm: Repair', 'draft', $t$The screaming has finally stopped. A two-year-old sits on the kitchen floor with wet cheeks and hiccups, and his father stands over him feeling two things at once, relief that it is over and guilt about the shouting in the middle of it.

This last lesson is about what happens next. We'll look at reconnecting after a meltdown, repairing after you lost your temper, talking about it later, and what the research says about the guilt, and then we'll close the class.

Lesson 4.1 showed that a tantrum ends in sadness that reaches for comfort. The researcher who mapped that shape points out that the distress draws the parent back in and mends what the anger broke, so reconnection is what a toddler's body is asking for at the end.

The Infant class showed that attachment is built from patterns of ordinary exchanges rather than from any one moment. A meltdown and its ending are one of those exchanges, and the ending is the part you have most say over.

The Infant class also gave the finding that matters most here. Filmed second by second, even happy, well-attached pairs were in step only about a third of the time, and what predicted security was the repair after each miss.

That finding has a toddler-age cousin. Researchers filmed ninety-six mothers and three-year-olds at a hard puzzle and counted how often a difficult moment, such as a refusal or a sharp word, was followed by the pair moving back into a good state together.

The pairs repaired about half of their difficult moments, and the more often a pair repaired, the better the child's emotion regulation and the fewer the behavior problems four months later. Half was ordinary, and more was better.

Lesson 3.2 described sixty-three mothers whose disagreements with their two-and-a-half-year-olds were recorded, and whose children understood feelings and right and wrong better six months later when the mothers gave reasons and brought disagreements to a real ending.

A second study of sixty-four pairs at thirty and thirty-six months found that securely attached toddlers had better-quality conflicts, with more resolution and compromise, but not fewer of them. What mattered was how they ended.

So the goal after a meltdown is not to have had no conflict. It is to end this one well.

Reconnection with a toddler is physical and brief. Come down to the floor, soften your face, and open your arms without pulling him in, because Lesson 4.3 showed that the turn from screaming to crying is the moment he can take comfort.

Say one thing about the feeling in his words, such as that was so hard, and you really wanted the blue cup, then carry on with the day, because a long talk now is for you rather than for him.

The limit stays where it was, since reconnecting is not undoing. He can be held, and he can still not have the blue cup, and Lesson 3.2 showed that the two go together in a warm limit.

Now the harder case, which is the shouting. In a national American survey, two thirds of parents of children from nineteen to thirty-five months yelled at their child often, and in another, nine in ten had used harsh words or threats by the time the child was two.

So if you shouted, you are in the majority, and the research on harm is about patterns. Among more than eight hundred toddlers in fourteen countries, shouting was linked with more behavior problems, in a British cohort of nearly seventeen thousand children persistent harsh discipline went with more problems over time, and across sixty countries no form of shouting or hitting helped children anywhere.

The pattern that matters is persistent, frightening, or contemptuous, and without repair. Harder children also draw harsher parenting, so it runs both ways, and spanking is the Preschool class's topic.

Repair with a toddler is short, honest, and in his words. You might say that you shouted, that it was too loud, that you are sorry, and that you are here now. Then the hug, if he wants it, and then the day.

Here is the honest line about the evidence, which is that no study has tested a parent apologizing to a toddler. Among a hundred and seventy-seven mothers of school-age children, those readier to apologize were warmer and more consistent, and had children who helped others more, though that is a survey that could run the other way.

Adolescents forgave a parent's apology more when it was about them rather than the parent, and a defensive apology did no better than none. So leave out the but, because sorry for shouting, but you would not put your shoes on, is not a repair.

Lesson 1.3 promised that toddlers learn from watching you recover and make things right, and the evidence comes mostly from how children respond to conflicts between adults.

In a diary study of a hundred and two mothers recording nearly six hundred conflicts at home, children settled when a conflict ended in compromise or apology and stayed unsettled when it ended in silence or withdrawal, and among two hundred and thirty-eight preschoolers, those who saw their parents handle disagreements constructively knew more about feelings a year later and had fewer problems after that.

In sixty-two families followed from two to ten, the lingering aftermath of unresolved conflict at two predicted anxiety at ten more than the argument itself. The Family Systems class takes up conflict between parents, and a child who sees you come back from anger learns that anger is survivable.

Should you talk about a meltdown afterward? With a toddler, briefly, and later. Among sixty-three mothers and their thirty-month-olds, each mother talked with her child about a past misbehavior and a past good behavior, and mothers who were clear, elaborated, and talked about feelings had children who understood feelings and right and wrong better six months later.

The story is simple and ends well. You wanted the cup, you got so mad, and then you took a breath and we found your shoes. Among three- to six-year-olds, mothers who dwelt on the negative in these talks had children who read new situations negatively, so end the story on the recovery.

Lesson 4.3 said to name the feeling later, and later is now. One word that fits, such as mad or sad, said with a warm face, is the whole job with a toddler.

Among two hundred and forty-two toddlers around two and a half, mothers who coached feelings had toddlers with better regulation, and mothers who dismissed them had toddlers with worse. The Preschool class teaches emotion coaching in full, with its three steps of accept the feeling, help name it, and talk about it once everyone is calm.

The toddler version is the same three steps with fewer words in each.

Parents feel guilty after a meltdown, and especially after shouting. Among a hundred and sixty-seven parents who each recalled a moment of parenting they felt guilty or ashamed about, half were then prompted to respond to themselves with the kindness they would offer a friend.

The prompted parents reported less guilt and shame afterward, and a review of thirteen parenting programs with a self-compassion element found lower parental stress, anxiety, and depression, although the studies were weak and the effects on children unclear.

The useful question after a storm is not what kind of parent am I, but what does my child need now. Among parents of one- to nine-year-olds, guilt weighed heaviest in those who reflected least on what was happening in the child's mind, and parental stress itself is the Family Systems class's topic.

Repair is a pattern, not a performance. A toddler does not need a perfect apology, and a long one is more than he can use. He needs to see, many times over, that the storm passes and you come back.

The ninety-four toddler study in Lesson 4.3 found that consistency over a year, not any single episode, predicted whether tantrums improved, and repairing about half of the difficult moments was the norm among the three-year-olds, so more than half is a good aim and every time is not the standard.

After repair, the day goes on, the limit stands, and the routine from Lesson 4.2 picks up where it left off.

Lesson 4.1 showed that tantrums fade on their own, from a peak in the second and third years to fewer than half of children by five or six, and almost nothing you do during a tantrum changes that curve.

What stays is the pattern around them. A child met with a steady body at the peak, a warm face at the turn, and a repair after the times it went wrong has learned something about big feelings that lasts long after the tantrums are gone.

That is the real work of this module, and it is done in the ordinary minutes after the storm rather than in the storm itself.

Here is the whole class in a few sentences. Module 1 gave you the toddler brain, with its early alarm and late brakes, the gap between knowing a rule and following it, and a child who learns most by watching you.

Module 2 gave you words, which arrive in a rush and in a wide range, grow from the back-and-forth of ordinary talk, and deserve a check when they are late.

Module 3 gave you the self, which says mine and no, needs choices inside warm limits, and wants to help, and this module gave you the storms, how to see them coming, how to be the calm in the room, and how to come back.

If there is one idea under all of it, it is this. A toddler borrows what she does not yet have. She borrows your calm when her alarm fires, your words when hers run out, your patience when hers is gone, and your way of coming back after a hard moment.

Every lesson in this class has been a version of lending well, not so much that she never practices, and not so little that she is left alone with equipment she does not have yet. The brakes grow through thousands of ordinary borrowings, and faster with a steady lender.

A few places in this class pointed to a professional. Lesson 2.3 gave the language and hearing route, Lesson 4.1 gave the tantrum signs, and this lesson adds you, because if most days end at the end of your rope, that is a reason to ask for help.

This class left out sleep, toilet training, picky eating, and screens after two on purpose, because they are practical questions well covered elsewhere.

And when your child turns three, the Preschool Years class picks up where this one leaves off, with the brakes beginning to work, feelings that can be named and coached, play with other children, and what readiness really means.

Here are three things to take with you.

Reconnect at the turn, briefly. Come down to the floor, offer the hug, name the feeling in one word, and carry on with the day, with the limit still in place.

Repair after you lose your temper. Most parents of toddlers shout sometimes, and a short, honest apology with no but in it is the model your child learns from.

Talk about it later, and end on the recovery. A short, warm story about what happened, finishing with how he calmed down, teaches more than any lecture at the peak.

Thank you for taking this class. The toddler years are loud, and they are also the years when a child learns, from you, that big feelings come and go and that people come back.

You will not get it right every time, and the research in this module says that you don't have to. Come back more often than not, and that is the whole of it.$t$)
